WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.945 --> 00:00:13.070
- Good afternoon, everybody
and welcome to today's events.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:14.020
My name is Douglas Rogers,

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.860
I teach in the Anthropology Department,

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.780
and I'm the Faculty
Director of the program

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.240
in Russian East European
and Eurasian studies.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.870
Today is a special event not
just because of our guest,

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:27.000
who's one of the most well
known, most distinguished

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.980
and most fascinating
journalists and broadcasters

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.167
in the past 50 years,
anywhere in the world,

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:35.200
but because in a much
smaller and more modest way,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.290
this is the first time that words program

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.290
in Russian East European
and Eurasian studies

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.093
are being uttered in public at Yale.

00:00:42.772 --> 00:00:47.220
Applause is appropriate.
(audience applauding)

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.830
Our program dates from July 1st, 2018.

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:53.900
And we think that the
interest that we're carrying

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.550
around campus and full
auditoriums like today

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.990
are good evidence of expanding
interest in the study

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.650
of this part of the world.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:03.810
So thank you for coming.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.010
Look at our website,
get on our newsletter,

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.500
we hope to bring you many
more events like this.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.410
I want to thank Macmillan Center,

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.050
the council in European studies,

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.460
for helping us with this event also,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.740
and especially the pointer
fellowship in journalism,

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.850
and the Office of Public
Affairs and communication.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.663
Eileen O'Connor is somewhere here.

00:01:23.662 --> 00:01:26.340
Thank you very much for your
partnership in this event

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.910
and many other events
we've had in the past

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.480
and hope to have in the future.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.360
The plan for today is
that I will turn things

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.450
over to Constantine Muravnik,
who I'm also very grateful to

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.930
for suggesting this program
and for doing a lot of the work

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.020
to make it happen.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.460
He'll introduce our guest,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.380
who will then speak for a little while,

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.160
followed by sort of open question answer

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.620
and discussion period.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.664
So without further ado, Constantine.

00:01:52.664 --> 00:01:55.831
(audience applauding)

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.550
- It is my distinct honor to welcome

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.333
Vladimir Pozner to Yale.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:08.770
Mr. Pozner needs an
introduction only for those

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:12.730
who are too young to remember
the '80s and the '90s

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.400
in this country when he regularly appeared

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.300
on Ted Koppel's Nightline
and The Phil Donahue Show

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.713
and then co hosted the
Pozner/Donahue show on CNBC.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.280
At that time, he once visited Yale,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.380
so it's not a welcome but a welcome back.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:33.710
Mr. Pozner may also need
an introduction to those

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.810
who are detached from contemporary Russia,

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.460
where he is omnipresent in the media.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.970
For the past 10 years, he has
hosted a weekly show, Pozner,

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.420
where he interviews various
national and world leaders

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:51.650
from all walks of life;
Gorbachev, Sekulow,

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.050
Gaidar, Shoigu, Ted Turner

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.930
and Vekselberg, Hillary Clinton and Sting,

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.640
Michael McFaul and Ksenia
Sobchak, among many others

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.980
had a chance to face Mr.
Pozner's thoughtful scrutiny

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:07.810
and be judged by millions
of Russian viewers.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:11.360
Mr. Pozner's opinions on
a wide range of questions

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.900
from politics to soccer,
from history to astronomy,

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.710
and arts quickly go viral.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:21.900
They have become a fact of
Russian life to such an extent

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.980
that he has been termed,
have seriously, half ingest,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.200
the spiritual leader of the
nation and its moral compass.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.520
This is despite the fact
that Mr. Pozner is Russian

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.240
just as much as he is
an American or French.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.760
He was born in France and
christened in Notre Dame,

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.460
grew up in the U.S. and came to Russia

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.173
only at the age of 19.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.290
Perhaps this cosmopolitan
aspect of his biography

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.670
is what endears him to Russian public tire

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.403
like any other public
of ideological agendas.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.530
Perhaps, this ability to
be simultaneously Russian

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.560
and American and European
attunes Mr. Pozner

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.830
to the subtleties of
different perspectives,

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.810
and brings him one step
closer to the much desired

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:16.810
and no less appreciated
objectivity and truth.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:20.440
Especially now, when the
gaps separating governments

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:23.750
and nations are only widening.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.750
And one side increasingly
refuses to consider

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:27.903
the views of the other.

00:04:29.145 --> 00:04:32.040
In his more than five decades
in the field of journalism,

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.770
Mr. Pozner has done his share

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:36.120
of partisanship and propaganda.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.830
However, this changed in the '80s

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:40.520
when he pioneered a project

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.510
of so called space bridges,
or tele bridges that connect

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:47.720
with Russian and American audiences.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.540
Moscow viewers named Kim TV
journalist number one in 1989.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.820
And this high mark of recognition

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.410
has never decreased ever since.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:02.410
In 1989, and in from 1994 to 2008,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.390
Mr. Pozner headed the
Russian television Academy.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:10.780
In 1997, he founded the School
for Television Excellence,

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:15.450
a platform for education and
promotion of young journalists.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:16.880
He's written several books

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:19.650
and made a number of
documentary miniseries

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:23.320
about different countries,
their cultures and people.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.160
These films brought the
U.S., France, Italy,

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.910
Germany, England, Israel and Spain closer

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:31.370
to millions of Russian people.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:35.470
It is my hope that this
conversation with Mr. Pozner at Yale

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:38.383
will also achieve what
he has been so good at.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.890
That it will bring
Russian views and opinions

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.620
a little closer to our
students and colleagues,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:48.270
and will maintain what
we all need now the most,

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:52.160
a dialogue, based on mutual
understanding and respect.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.889
So please join me in
welcoming Vladimir Pozner.

00:05:54.889 --> 00:05:58.056
(audience applauding)

00:06:00.265 --> 00:06:02.150
- Thank you very much.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.433
Quite an introduction.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.170
I'd like to say a couple
of words about who I am

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:18.023
and what I am not withstanding
what we just heard.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.500
It's important that you understand

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.500
that I don't represent
anybody or anything,

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:32.850
any organization,
political, social, whatever.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.653
I represent myself.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.220
I am an independent journalist.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:43.583
And that's an animal that
is disappearing in Russia

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:46.403
and not only in Russia.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:54.280
I think, for me, it's
important that I say that

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:59.370
and I hope I'm not going to speak long

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:02.580
because I was told we would
have a conversation afterwards.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:04.940
And I think that might be the
most interesting part of it

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:09.090
because you have questions or views

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.020
that you might wanna share with me,

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:12.820
and I can't guess them in advance.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:15.380
But there are certain
things I'd like to say

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.210
before we have that conversation.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.793
I'd like to say, first
of all, that we are,

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.533
at an extremely dangerous moment today.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.870
Never have the relations between Russia

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:32.210
and the United States
with the Soviet Union,

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:37.120
not what it was before,
been at this level.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.580
During the worst times of the Cold War,

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.910
when I was living in the Soviet Union,

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.683
and I remember all that very, very well.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:50.530
Russians were anti White
House, anti Wall Street,

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:57.160
but not anti American,
in their vast majority.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:01.140
In fact, there was a
kind of a warm feeling

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:02.533
these are the Americans.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:05.880
Today that's different.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:09.883
Today it's anti American
at the grassroots level.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:13.070
And there's a reason for it.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:18.410
Another thing that is, to me scary

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:22.970
is that neither side seems to be afraid

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.393
of nuclear weapons.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:30.590
30 years ago, those of
you who are of my age

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:34.920
certainly remember an American
movie called "The Day After,"

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:38.170
which is about what happens
to you and to your country

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:39.883
after a nuclear strike.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.610
There was fear of these weapons

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:46.800
as there was in the Soviet
Union, there was a realization

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.800
that these weapons can and if
used will destroy our country.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.190
Today, there's a feeling
when you talk to people,

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:57.770
it's as if there are no nuclear weapons.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:02.770
It really doesn't seem to
play a role in how we act.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.000
And the danger of a not a
deliberate nuclear exchange,

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:14.880
but an accidental one has
grown because the level

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:18.173
of mistrust between the two
countries has grown as well.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.670
There have been several times in the past

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:27.123
when computers warned of a nuclear attack.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:31.470
But it never got to the real thing

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:35.873
because people took the
time to really check it out.

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.730
Now, they didn't have a long time.

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:42.550
If an ICBM is launched from Russia,

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:45.850
it will take about 10 minutes
for it to hit the U.S.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:48.153
So you know, and vice versa obviously.

00:09:50.807 --> 00:09:53.960
So you don't have a long
time but you do have some.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.410
But my feeling is that if today

00:09:55.410 --> 00:10:00.410
those same computers malfunction
and it's on either side,

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:03.580
that an attack has been launched,

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:05.180
the response would be immediate.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.123
Because the feeling is that
this is what's going to happen.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:17.893
Not that long ago, we were all
very optimistic, weren't we?

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:23.420
Gorbachev, Gorby, Gorby,
Russians, we're gonna be friends,

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:24.330
we're gonna be...

00:10:25.466 --> 00:10:28.300
And in such a really short period of time,

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:29.513
how did this happen?

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.890
Why are we at the point that we are today?

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.230
And I'm not saying who's to blame,

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:39.820
because that's not a productive
way of looking at things.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.913
But we should try to understand
exactly what did happen.

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:51.453
The Soviet Union once Gorbachev took over,

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.110
didn't really last very long.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:58.310
He came to power in March of 1985.

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:02.980
And by December 1991, there
was no more Soviet Union.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:06.863
Some people say, it
collapsed, didn't collapse.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:10.397
In a place called the Belavezha,

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:14.680
which is a kind of a
forest, three presidents,

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:18.360
the President of Ukraine,
the president of Belarus,

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:22.697
and the President of Russia
proper, Mr. Yeltsin decided

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:28.900
to part company, decided to
disband the Soviet Union.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:34.510
Now, each had his own reasons, definitely.

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:38.740
But if we look at Mr. Yeltsin,
his reasoning was very clear.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:41.350
He was the president of Russia.

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:44.760
So he was number two to Gorbachev.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:48.400
Because Gorbachev was
president of the Soviet Union,

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:53.210
which Russia was part, the
largest part, but only part.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:55.680
Get rid of the Soviet Union,

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:00.590
and there's no president and
you get rid of Gorbachev.

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:02.573
That's precisely what he did.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:06.640
So no more Soviet Union.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.080
Quickly no more Warsaw Pact,
of course, that is to say,

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:14.710
countries that were usually
called Soviet satellites,

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:17.600
and part of a military
alliance with the Soviet Union,

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:18.953
that Alliance disappeared.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:24.410
And so the United States
had to figure out,

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:28.303
how do we deal with this
new entity called Russia?

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:30.580
How do we deal with it?

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.820
There's no more Soviet Union.

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:37.820
What is going to be U.S.
policy, vis-a-vis this country.

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:43.660
And of course, Yeltsin also had to think

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:46.940
about what is going to
be Russia's attitude

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.923
towards the United States.

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:57.780
You may remember that soon
after the Soviet Union

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:02.610
cease to exist, and I think
it was February of 1992,

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.640
Yeltsin came to the United States.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:13.500
And he addressed the
joint session of Congress.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:17.620
And he said, the people of
Russia are offering their hand

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:21.610
to the people of the United
States in friendship,

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:25.470
to build a better world,
a world without war,

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:27.780
a world with our peace.

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:30.940
And this was exactly
what the vast majority

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:32.643
of Russians wanted.

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:37.530
And I would even say that
today, the vast majority

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:41.400
of Russians would like to
have, if not a friendship

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.750
with the United States,
at least a partnership.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:48.463
There's no doubt to my
mind that that's the case.

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:53.107
So that was what Yeltsin wanted,

00:13:53.107 --> 00:13:58.077
and what kind of response did he get?

00:13:58.077 --> 00:14:01.600
What kind of response did Russia get?

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:06.600
Well, the United States
could have picked two ways

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:08.403
of treating Russia.

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:12.450
One was to say, let's treat
Russia like we did our enemies

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:17.450
after World War II, Germany, Italy,

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.430
and in some of the countries
that were occupied,

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:24.320
such as France, or were
not occupied such as UK

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:26.063
but were really badly hurt.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:31.340
Let's find a way to see to
it that in those countries,

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:34.123
Nazis, fascists do not come back.

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:38.070
And communists do not come
to power, and may remind you

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:41.720
that in those days, the
Communist Party of France

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:44.823
and the Communist Party of
Italy were very, very powerful.

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:50.723
And that plan turned out was
called later the Marshall Plan,

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:56.800
which was basically a
financial ideas idea to spend

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:01.490
a lot of money but in a very precise way

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:03.770
to develop certain things,

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:06.910
and not to allow others to develop.

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:11.910
Now, that could be the policy,
to adopt vis-a-vis Russia.

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:17.800
See to it that democracy begins
to develop in that country.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.210
And let me say, just for the record,

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:24.060
Russia never in its entire thousand years,

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:28.763
never had democracy, completely absent.

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:33.090
So it wasn't like something
that once upon a time

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:36.430
Russians had, and then they
lost, but they knew what it was.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:37.880
They didn't know what it was.

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:44.580
So let's spend money on getting
democracy moving in Russia

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:49.663
and seeing to it that the
communists do not get back.

00:15:50.937 --> 00:15:53.170
And that could have been one approach.

00:15:53.170 --> 00:15:58.170
The other approach was
to say, for 40 years,

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:02.700
you held a nuclear bomb over our heads,

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:05.993
you lost the Cold War, and
you're gonna pay for it.

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:10.123
You're gonna be punished for what you did.

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:15.870
And there were people who
supported one view and people

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:18.023
who supported the other in this country.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:27.170
Early in 1992 a document was
produced in the United States

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:30.983
by a gentleman called Paul Wolfowitz.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:36.830
You may know who he was, he
was under Secretary of Defense

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:40.153
of the United States
responsible for policy.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:44.760
The document he produced came to be called

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:48.990
the Wealth of its
Doctrine, not officially,

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.000
but that's the way it was addressed.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.083
It later was incorporated in something

00:16:53.083 --> 00:16:55.333
that was officially
called the Bush Doctrine.

00:16:56.840 --> 00:17:00.290
That document was leaked
to the New York Times.

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:02.740
And so it became public.

00:17:02.740 --> 00:17:05.390
And what it basically said,
and you can look it up,

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:09.610
it's available, you know,
just go to Wolfowitz Doctrine,

00:17:09.610 --> 00:17:13.063
and you'll find it, what
it basically said was this.

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:17.010
The United States should never again,

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:20.533
allow any other country to challenge it.

00:17:22.220 --> 00:17:27.070
The United States must
remain the superior country.

00:17:27.070 --> 00:17:29.050
And we should tell our allies not to worry

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:31.210
about developing their own weapons

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:33.453
because we will do that for them.

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:36.550
And we must watch out for Russia

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:40.550
because we don't know
which way it's going to go.

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:45.283
The bear might get up on his
hind legs again, and growl.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:50.930
When that document was
leaked to the New York Times,

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:55.930
there was an outcry by the
more liberal, if you will,

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:00.960
in America now the word
liberal and conservative

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:04.180
has lost the meaning that
it once upon a time had.

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:06.140
So when I say liberal, I'm not sure

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:08.260
that I'm saying the right word,

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:11.363
but at least many people
were upset by this document.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:17.240
Edward Kennedy said that it
was an imperialist document

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:20.473
that no country could or should accept.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.990
It was quickly as it were removed

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:29.990
and rewritten by Mr. Cheney.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:33.200
Not a very liberal man in any sense

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:35.150
as far as I can remember.

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:40.150
And the Secretary of Defense
in those days, Mr. Powell,

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:49.780
but basically it retained
that view, Russia and America

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:54.323
must remain, was to be
the only superpower.

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:01.193
And basically, that view was
the one that was accepted.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:04.730
It was the one that was accepted.

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:08.170
And the attitude towards
Russia was pretty much,

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:11.103
you're no longer a superpower.

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:14.183
You are a second rate country.

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:19.963
Just keep quiet, please.

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:27.313
This became evident and
would be evident to you

00:19:29.110 --> 00:19:32.790
if you follow the policy
of the United States.

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:36.670
Now, let's begin with
going back to Gorbachev

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:41.670
and his meetings when he
was asked by several people,

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:47.440
all of them quite important,
to allow Germany's to reunite

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:51.230
and take down the Berlin Wall.

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:55.940
And he was told by James Baker,

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:59.200
and now this is not many people.

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:00.340
I mean, when I would say this,

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:02.900
many people say, it's
not true, it's not true.

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:06.393
He was told by James
Baker, if this happens,

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:11.070
NATO will not move one inch eastward.

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:14.723
Well, not long ago, on
December 12th, 2017,

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:19.500
the National Defense archives

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:21.830
of George Washington University,

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:26.210
declassified the minutes of
the baker Gorbachev discussion,

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:27.043
and it's there.

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:30.300
But it's not only Baker
who said that to him,

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:32.410
there was several people there,

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:33.970
the German leadership did,

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:35.820
Western Germany those days and so on.

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:42.490
And finally, I'm not saying,
I don't know whether Gorbachev

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:44.850
could have stopped Germany from uniting

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:47.500
but the fact of the matter
is that they said yes

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:49.953
and took down the Berlin Wall.

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:56.520
And NATO stayed put, it
stayed put in those days.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:59.483
It stayed put under Bush Senior,

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:04.693
it stayed put during the
first four years of Clinton.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:09.570
But in the next four years
in 1996, approximately,

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:14.390
a decision was taken to enlarge NATO,

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:20.810
three countries; Poland, the
Czech Republic, and Hungary.

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:24.497
Now, I'm gonna read something to you.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:27.500
You know who Thomas Friedman is?

00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:31.863
New York Times old hand columnist.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:37.470
He, when this happened, this
is already in 19, this is 1998.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:40.570
He called up George Kennan.

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:43.150
I don't know if you're all
aware of who George Kennan was,

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:44.910
but he was one, in my opinion,

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:46.850
perhaps one of the most brilliant minds,

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:49.280
political minds of the United States

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:51.600
in the second half of the 20th century.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.900
The man who devised
the idea of containment

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:57.200
of the Soviet Union rather than war

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.820
against the Soviet Union,
successfully did this.

00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:03.080
So, you know, a brilliant man

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:08.080
who established the very
foundation of U.S. policy,

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:10.900
this will be the Soviet Union.

00:22:10.900 --> 00:22:13.823
So Thomas Friedman called him up.

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:17.560
The article he published
in the New York Times,

00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:22.560
it's called foreign affairs,
now a word from x, why x?

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:27.750
Because in 1947, in the
magazine Foreign Affairs

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:34.690
Mr. Kennan had published this
article about containment,

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:36.530
and he signed it x.

00:22:36.530 --> 00:22:39.120
So he called him up and he asked him,

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:43.800
what did he think about his
decision to enlarge NATO?

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:48.533
Let me quote, "I think
this is May 2nd, 1998.

00:22:49.957 --> 00:22:54.297
"I think it is the
beginning of a new Cold War,

00:22:54.297 --> 00:22:57.637
"said Mr. Kennan from his Princeton home.

00:22:57.637 --> 00:23:02.277
"I think the Russians will
gradually react quite adversely,

00:23:02.277 --> 00:23:05.107
"and it will affect their policies.

00:23:05.107 --> 00:23:07.653
"I think it is a tragic mistake.

00:23:08.597 --> 00:23:11.957
"There was no reason for this whatsoever."

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:19.340
That decision, and now
I'm giving you my opinion,

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:22.113
is what really started this relation,

00:23:26.232 --> 00:23:28.260
turning itself, as you might say,

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:30.283
that's where it all began.

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:35.270
Because the Russian reaction
and specifically this is 1998,

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:39.767
So this is Yeltsin, late Yeltsin was,

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:44.373
you promised not to do this.

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:50.780
So, how do we trust you,
if you make a promise?

00:23:50.780 --> 00:23:52.900
I would also like you to,

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:57.150
perhaps try to solve a little problem.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:24:00.490
It's a kind of a mathematical.

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:04.640
Take the time from when
Gorbachev came to power,

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:09.640
March 1985, to 2007, when Putin

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:14.883
has been in power for seven
years, that's 22 years.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:18.630
I ask you to find a single thing

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:24.970
in foreign or domestic policies
done by the Soviet Union

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:28.890
while still existed,
and then Russia proper

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:33.890
that might in any way anger, irk,

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:37.683
disappoint the United States.

00:24:39.610 --> 00:24:41.290
Let me answer that for you.

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:45.973
Nothing, not one thing during that period.

00:24:47.410 --> 00:24:51.520
Now, what did Russia
get as a result of that?

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:53.833
First, the enlargement of NATO.

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.203
So that was number one.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:59.590
Then the bombing of Yugoslavia.

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:03.910
That was done by NATO and
NATO is after all dependent

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:05.900
mostly on the United States.

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:07.123
Let's face it, right?

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:10.540
The UN did not condone this.

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:15.540
So the bombing of Yugoslavia
that's from March 24th,'99,

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:17.943
to June 10th, '99.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:24.000
Then Kosovo and recognition of Kosovo

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:29.140
although it had been part
of Serbia for centuries,

00:25:29.500 --> 00:25:31.800
and there were people in Russia who said,

00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:34.673
you're letting the genie out of the bottle.

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:37.167
Because if you do this, then
there are other countries

00:25:37.167 --> 00:25:39.250
that will do the same.

00:25:39.250 --> 00:25:41.360
And Russia did the same

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:44.333
as the Aphasia to begin with, okay.

00:25:50.826 --> 00:25:53.190
Yelstin was very angry.

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:55.570
He made a speech, he said, and of course,

00:25:55.570 --> 00:25:56.870
this is very Yeltsin-like.

00:25:56.870 --> 00:26:01.870
He said, "We're not Haiti,
you can't treat us like Haiti.

00:26:02.037 --> 00:26:04.497
"Were a great country.

00:26:04.497 --> 00:26:09.023
"We have a great past and
Russia will come back.

00:26:10.047 --> 00:26:12.020
"Russia will come back."

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:14.533
He was really, really angry.

00:26:15.607 --> 00:26:17.860
He didn't say the
politically correct thing

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:19.123
but he spoke his mind.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:29.230
Then finally 2000, year 2000,
Mr. Putin is not elected,

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:35.510
although elected to the presidency.

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:40.200
And one of the first thing
he does is to ask for Russia,

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:43.913
to become a member of NATO.

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:48.690
Why not be a member of NATO?

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:52.300
NATO was created to defend Europe

00:26:52.300 --> 00:26:57.300
and perhaps not only Europe
from Soviet aggression,

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:01.580
from a country that you couldn't predict.

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:03.490
There is no more Soviet Union,

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:05.830
and there is no more Warsaw Pact.

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:08.880
Why can't we create an organization

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:11.570
where we are part of it, said Mr. Putin,

00:27:11.570 --> 00:27:16.570
and act together to protect
from some kind of aggression.

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:23.400
He was told, go take a walk, basically.

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:29.050
What about some kind of partnership

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:31.233
or becoming part of the European Union?

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:36.700
Again, and this is all
documented, everything I say,

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:40.200
except when I say my
opinion, is documented,

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:41.253
you can look it up.

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:45.623
And he said, no, you know, you're too big.

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:48.743
Your country is too big, you can.

00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:56.430
And all the while Russia
was being reminded

00:27:56.430 --> 00:28:01.053
that it's no longer really
that important in the country.

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:05.910
Now, one of the things
you must keep in mind

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:11.060
is that much like the
Americans, the Russians believe

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:16.040
that they have a mission,
that their country

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:18.350
was selected by destiny.

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:21.320
Now, you know, my being
French I laugh at that.

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:23.790
I laugh both at you and at them.

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:26.957
Because we French know that
we're the best and we are known,

00:28:26.957 --> 00:28:30.540
and we have no mission,
you know, that's it.

00:28:30.540 --> 00:28:33.453
But seriously speaking, that's a fact.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:39.527
And so the sense of losing
this aura of greatness

00:28:43.700 --> 00:28:46.793
of being told, we don't care about you,

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:52.710
the reaction of the
average Russian to that

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:56.000
was one of you're insulting me.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.410
You don't respect me.

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:01.430
And so the anger, gradually,

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:05.250
and the anger focused on Gorbachev.

00:29:05.250 --> 00:29:08.760
Many, many Russians figured
you sold the country.

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:10.453
You don't stand up to these men,

00:29:10.453 --> 00:29:12.770
to the United States.

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:15.450
And then the same thing for Yeltsin.

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:19.330
You'd be surprised how unpopular Gorbachev

00:29:19.330 --> 00:29:24.133
and Yeltsin are today in
Russia, maybe 5% support them,

00:29:26.100 --> 00:29:27.700
precisely for that reason?

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:28.890
Well, there are some others as well

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:32.460
that have to do with economic
things, but nonetheless.

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:34.760
So now here we have Putin,

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:38.990
who as you know, as soon as 911 happens,

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:43.990
calls up, Bush Jr. W, and offers his help.

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:49.210
And yes, and does help in Afghanistan.

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:51.010
And if you wanna have your soldiers,

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:55.600
your military people in Central
Asia, right on our borders,

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:58.790
be my guest and in Georgia, absolutely.

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:01.290
So it's not just words, you know,

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:03.400
we wanna fight terrorism together

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:11.560
and gets nothing in exchange.

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:16.560
So finally in 2007, in
Munich speaking to the 20,

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:26.363
the group of 20 in
Munich, Putin says this.

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:28.963
This is February 10th.

00:30:30.117 --> 00:30:32.357
"I think it is obvious that NATO expansion

00:30:32.357 --> 00:30:34.717
"does not have any relation

00:30:34.717 --> 00:30:37.507
"with the modernization
of the Alliance itself

00:30:37.507 --> 00:30:40.857
"or with ensuring security in Europe.

00:30:40.857 --> 00:30:44.657
"On the contrary, it represents
a serious provocation

00:30:44.657 --> 00:30:48.457
"that reduces the level of mutual trust.

00:30:48.457 --> 00:30:52.337
"And we have the right to ask against whom

00:30:52.337 --> 00:30:54.803
"Is this expansion intended?

00:30:56.217 --> 00:30:59.477
"And what happened to the
assurance of our Western partners

00:30:59.477 --> 00:31:03.967
"made after the dissolution
of the Warsaw Pact,

00:31:03.967 --> 00:31:06.383
"where are those declarations today?

00:31:07.557 --> 00:31:09.803
"No one even remembers them.

00:31:10.807 --> 00:31:13.617
"But I will allow myself
to remind this audience

00:31:13.617 --> 00:31:14.743
"what was said.

00:31:16.217 --> 00:31:18.727
"I would like to quote the
speech of General Secretary,

00:31:18.727 --> 00:31:23.727
"Mr. Berner of Brussels on May 17th, 1990.

00:31:24.607 --> 00:31:29.607
"He said at the time, quote,
the fact that we are not ready

00:31:30.197 --> 00:31:34.847
"to place a NATO army
outside of German territory,

00:31:34.847 --> 00:31:38.653
"gives the Soviet Union a
firm security guarantee.

00:31:39.787 --> 00:31:42.330
"Where are these guarantees?"

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:44.790
And you know what the answer was?

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:47.290
The answer was, yes, but
that was guarantees given

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.993
to the Soviet Union and you're Russia.

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:54.650
Well, what kind of a
reaction would you expect?

00:31:57.920 --> 00:32:00.100
Last year, I think it was,

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:02.783
making a foreign policy
speech, Putin said,

00:32:04.667 --> 00:32:07.323
"Our mistake was that
we trusted you too much.

00:32:08.867 --> 00:32:10.307
"And your mistake was that you tried

00:32:10.307 --> 00:32:11.707
"to take advantage of that."

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:15.340
That is the situation today.

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:18.930
Now, it may seem to you that
I'm blaming the United States,

00:32:18.930 --> 00:32:21.440
I don't want the word blame used.

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:24.183
It was a mistaken political decision.

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:28.050
It was not the Russians.

00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:33.050
It was this decision that
finally led to this change

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:38.000
in Putin's attitude towards
the west and in particular,

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:39.010
towards the United States,

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:41.710
which is why I say how U.S. policy

00:32:41.710 --> 00:32:46.610
created Putin the way he is today.

00:32:46.610 --> 00:32:50.217
And the really, if you
will, dangerous thing

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:58.140
is that Russian leadership,
I should be more precise

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:03.013
and say Vladimir Putin
does not trust the West,

00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:06.960
does not trust the United States,

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:10.780
which makes it very difficult to move away

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:12.343
from where we are today.

00:33:13.520 --> 00:33:17.120
So something I wanna underline,

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:21.097
so we are in now in a new
arms race, which is terrible.

00:33:24.980 --> 00:33:29.980
We are in a new Cold War
which threatens all of us.

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:36.513
The danger of an accidental
nuclear exchange has grown.

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:39.840
We no longer seem to fear that,

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:42.130
there used to be demonstrations, you know,

00:33:42.130 --> 00:33:44.980
get rid of nuclear weapons,
that's not happening anymore.

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:51.930
The possibility of a terrorist
organization somehow getting

00:33:51.930 --> 00:33:54.993
a nuclear weapon has grown.

00:33:56.820 --> 00:33:59.390
And to make it look like someone used it

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:01.713
on each side, not the terrorists.

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:08.570
So that I believe is something
we should all understand

00:34:10.060 --> 00:34:13.163
and finally, as someone
who works in media,

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:18.243
I would like to say that
Russian media, Mainstream,

00:34:19.858 --> 00:34:24.607
I mean Mainstream Media,
paints America black

00:34:27.830 --> 00:34:31.990
Russian media, Mainstream
media controlled directly

00:34:31.990 --> 00:34:33.710
or indirectly by the government

00:34:36.430 --> 00:34:41.430
shows an extremely negative
picture of the United States,

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:43.293
U.S. policy and so on.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:49.680
And much to my surprise,
mainstream American media

00:34:49.680 --> 00:34:52.373
does exactly the same
thing vis-a-vis Russia.

00:34:53.490 --> 00:34:57.690
Which to me, is amazing
because this is supposed

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:02.400
to be a free media that's
differing from the Russian one.

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:03.850
As someone who works in Russian media,

00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:06.573
I can say it's hard to
call it a free media.

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:10.540
There are some opposition
newspapers and radio,

00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:12.490
but that's not mainstream.

00:35:12.490 --> 00:35:14.853
They address a very
small number of people.

00:35:17.350 --> 00:35:20.100
So there we are, I think people

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:23.180
who call themselves
journalists in my book,

00:35:23.180 --> 00:35:25.720
they're not journalists.

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:28.670
But those people have
played and are playing

00:35:29.730 --> 00:35:34.730
a destructive role in creating
the fear, the dislike,

00:35:35.470 --> 00:35:39.360
the distrust, that the
people in both countries

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:42.050
have vis-a-vis each other.

00:35:42.050 --> 00:35:46.160
And the fact that we don't
seem to question our media

00:35:47.410 --> 00:35:49.313
is really quite interesting.

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:53.703
But there it is,
nonetheless, we just take it.

00:35:54.970 --> 00:35:59.970
So I'd like to wind up with
a quote from a gentleman,

00:36:00.610 --> 00:36:03.330
no, I hesitate to use the word gentleman,

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:06.563
from a man whose name was Herman Goering.

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:09.700
You all know who he was?

00:36:09.700 --> 00:36:12.590
There may be some people
who are too young to know.

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:17.590
Well, he was Hitler's right hand man,

00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:21.793
and he commanded the Luftwaffe,
the German Air Force.

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:25.240
And he was, of course, at Nuremberg,

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:27.883
he was judged, getting
sentenced to death to hang.

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.743
But he managed to get some poison,

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:35.700
probably from the Soviets, of course,

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:37.603
since they poison people, as we know,

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:40.963
so as not to be hanged.

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:46.000
But he was interviewed
by an American journalist

00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:48.373
shortly before he committed suicide.

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:51.793
And here's what he said.

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:56.327
And I think this is something
that we should all remember.

00:36:56.327 --> 00:36:58.933
"Naturally, the common
people don't want war.

00:37:00.396 --> 00:37:01.967
'Neither in Russia nor in England,

00:37:01.967 --> 00:37:06.133
"nor for that matter in
Germany, that is understood.

00:37:07.057 --> 00:37:09.547
"But after all, it is the
leaders of the country

00:37:09.547 --> 00:37:11.547
"who determined the policy.

00:37:11.547 --> 00:37:15.687
"And it is always a simple
matter to drag the people along,

00:37:15.687 --> 00:37:20.687
"whether it is the democracy
or a fascist dictatorship,

00:37:21.657 --> 00:37:24.943
"or a parliament, or a
communist dictatorship.

00:37:26.707 --> 00:37:30.847
"Voice or no voice, the
people can always be brought

00:37:30.847 --> 00:37:33.363
"to the bidding of the leaders.

00:37:34.467 --> 00:37:36.233
"That is easy.

00:37:37.247 --> 00:37:40.147
"All you have to do is tell them

00:37:40.147 --> 00:37:45.147
"they are being attacked
and denounce the peacemakers

00:37:46.017 --> 00:37:47.917
"for a lack of patriotism,

00:37:47.917 --> 00:37:51.077
"and exposing the country to danger.

00:37:51.077 --> 00:37:54.547
"It works the same in any country."

00:37:55.610 --> 00:37:57.223
Said Mr. Gary.

00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:00.030
And I think he was absolutely right.

00:38:01.090 --> 00:38:06.090
And we are being led by our
media, by our politicians

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:12.163
in that direction in both countries.

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:18.170
I remember an ad that I
saw, a famous American actor

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:23.773
whose name I, he starred in,
oh, gosh, so many movies,

00:38:24.978 --> 00:38:29.978
An African American, not a young, what?

00:38:31.945 --> 00:38:32.778
- [Woman] Freeman.

00:38:32.778 --> 00:38:37.660
- Freeman, yes, yes, and
he says, we are at war.

00:38:37.660 --> 00:38:38.560
And he does it very well.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:40.290
He's a wonderful actor.

00:38:40.290 --> 00:38:43.270
But you know, he's tells you,

00:38:43.270 --> 00:38:46.013
we are at war, and we
must and dah dah dah dah.

00:38:47.180 --> 00:38:48.730
And of course, it's very scary.

00:38:51.492 --> 00:38:55.567
You know, there's nothing I can
do about that except, speak.

00:38:57.160 --> 00:39:00.890
And I speak, I'm happy
to speak here today,

00:39:00.890 --> 00:39:02.243
I do this in Russia.

00:39:03.350 --> 00:39:06.500
And I'll keep doing it as
long as I possibly can.

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:09.740
Because there has to be some voice raised

00:39:09.740 --> 00:39:10.990
against what's happening.

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:13.773
We're being manipulated.

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:16.720
You know, the way Putin is portrayed.

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:18.170
Well, he's worse than Hitler.

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:24.020
And even Hillary Clinton,
you compare him to Hitler,

00:39:24.020 --> 00:39:27.600
this is, I'm not a Putin fan, believe me,

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:30.490
but what's going on here?

00:39:30.490 --> 00:39:35.490
And of course, President Trump,
well, even your own press

00:39:36.150 --> 00:39:41.150
is not too positive about him, but anyway.

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:44.540
So basically, that's what
I wanted to share with you

00:39:44.540 --> 00:39:47.250
and see I've only spoken for 45 minutes.

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:51.270
And I would very much like to discuss,

00:39:51.270 --> 00:39:54.945
I hope you have problems,
and problems, point.

00:39:54.945 --> 00:39:56.760
(audience laughing)

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:58.583
That was a Freudian slip, wasn't it?

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:03.600
I hope you have questions,
I'm sure that, you know,

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:08.260
what I've just said, in the
sense of what's happened,

00:40:08.260 --> 00:40:10.040
it can't be argued about.

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:14.190
I mean these are facts,
but how you interpret them,

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:15.790
that's a whole different issue.

00:40:15.790 --> 00:40:20.790
So thank you again for, for
listening and let's talk.

00:40:21.199 --> 00:40:24.366
(audience applauding)

00:40:32.847 --> 00:40:35.340
- So I am acutely aware of which one of us

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:38.530
has more experience in
this kind of a forum.

00:40:38.530 --> 00:40:41.510
So I think I will skip
the part where I ask

00:40:41.510 --> 00:40:43.704
the first question and
open it up for questions

00:40:43.704 --> 00:40:48.400
and comments with the following
suggestions and guidelines.

00:40:48.400 --> 00:40:52.420
We have two microphones,
please wait for a microphone.

00:40:52.420 --> 00:40:53.750
You may think that you have a loud voice

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:55.300
but the people behind you will not agree.

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:57.570
So please wait for the microphone.

00:40:57.570 --> 00:41:01.530
Please be aware of the fact
that we have some limited time

00:41:01.530 --> 00:41:02.840
for conversation and a lot of us.

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:05.580
So please keep your
questions reasonably brief

00:41:05.580 --> 00:41:07.050
and we'll try to keep things moving.

00:41:07.050 --> 00:41:10.860
And we'll move back and
forth from side to side.

00:41:10.860 --> 00:41:13.733
So I'm gonna start on this side.

00:41:15.670 --> 00:41:16.503
Go ahead, sir.

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:23.020
- I'm very interested in your
take on Russian interference.

00:41:27.210 --> 00:41:31.190
The U.S. Intelligence
overwhelmingly concluded that Russia

00:41:31.190 --> 00:41:34.000
is involved in U.S. elections.

00:41:34.000 --> 00:41:36.580
And I don't think I'm
over assuming in saying

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:40.980
that the Russians are in
favor of the Republicans.

00:41:40.980 --> 00:41:43.890
So I'm very interested
in your take on that.

00:41:43.890 --> 00:41:45.003
- Well, I think that's a you know,

00:41:45.003 --> 00:41:47.110
a question a lot of people have,

00:41:47.110 --> 00:41:49.200
I'm happy to answer as well as I can.

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:53.210
I wouldn't say that the Russians
support the Republicans.

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:54.517
I mean, the Russians don't really know

00:41:54.517 --> 00:41:56.003
who the republicans are.

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:59.610
It's not part of their daily interest

00:41:59.610 --> 00:42:02.170
or the democrats for that matter.

00:42:02.170 --> 00:42:05.152
But, is this microphone working?

00:42:05.152 --> 00:42:05.985
- [Audience] Yes.

00:42:05.985 --> 00:42:10.985
- Okay, but it is the fact
that Republican presidents

00:42:11.250 --> 00:42:12.620
over the years have been the ones

00:42:12.620 --> 00:42:15.660
that have achieved breakthroughs
with the Soviet Union.

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:20.083
Nixon did when Jews were allowed
to emigrate, this was 1972.

00:42:22.320 --> 00:42:26.460
Bush did, Reagan did,
while the democratic ones

00:42:26.460 --> 00:42:31.460
such as Carter, and Clinton
had not been so successful,

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:36.050
but that's, Russians did support,

00:42:36.050 --> 00:42:38.550
including the Russian
leadership I'm certain

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:39.643
did support Trump.

00:42:40.840 --> 00:42:42.653
Now, look, they had a choice.

00:42:43.740 --> 00:42:47.413
Hillary Clinton or Donald Trump.

00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:53.213
Hillary Clinton said
that Putin was a former

00:42:55.670 --> 00:43:00.670
KGB agent and had no soul
and compared him to Hitler.

00:43:05.420 --> 00:43:08.163
Trump said, I think I
can work with this man.

00:43:09.130 --> 00:43:10.300
I think we can do this.

00:43:10.300 --> 00:43:15.203
Now, yes, the Russians wanted him to win.

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:18.673
And I think that's normal.

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:21.840
Did the Russians interfere?

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:28.270
Officially, Putin denies it.

00:43:28.270 --> 00:43:30.143
The Russians officially deny it.

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:38.030
U.S. Intelligence,
different intelligence says,

00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:40.610
it can't furnish the exact proof

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:43.970
but it is certain that they did.

00:43:43.970 --> 00:43:46.790
I've been reading just recently, you know,

00:43:46.790 --> 00:43:51.180
there was this huge, I
don't know how many pages,

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:54.730
special issue in New York Times

00:43:54.730 --> 00:43:56.860
about the you know, pages and pages

00:43:56.860 --> 00:44:01.860
with wonderful illustrations

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:06.870
that are supposed to really
prove, you know, like this.

00:44:06.870 --> 00:44:08.813
No, this kind of thing.

00:44:09.730 --> 00:44:10.733
This is journalism.

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:12.793
Interesting, isn't it?

00:44:14.660 --> 00:44:17.800
And when you read the
article very carefully,

00:44:17.800 --> 00:44:19.670
and you ask yourself, it's not an article.

00:44:19.670 --> 00:44:24.670
This one, two, three, four with graphics,

00:44:25.330 --> 00:44:30.330
five, six, six pages
of the New York Times.

00:44:32.570 --> 00:44:35.963
And when you read it
carefully, you ask yourself,

00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:38.983
why was it published now?

00:44:40.150 --> 00:44:41.810
It's got to be a reason for this, right?

00:44:41.810 --> 00:44:44.300
I mean, this happened a while ago.

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:46.170
What is the idea?

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:49.500
And what you find at the very tail end

00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:56.000
is that over the past few
years in this country,

00:44:58.230 --> 00:45:02.473
the popularity of Mr.
Putin has increased by 11%.

00:45:05.440 --> 00:45:06.663
And the number of people who believe

00:45:06.663 --> 00:45:09.883
that the Russians interfered
has gone down by five.

00:45:11.760 --> 00:45:14.900
That's a good reason to
publish something like this.

00:45:14.900 --> 00:45:18.520
And this is a very
detailed, interesting story

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:23.230
that doesn't furnish any proof.

00:45:23.230 --> 00:45:25.900
But it's very well done as
the New York Times does.

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:26.960
This is just one.

00:45:26.960 --> 00:45:29.143
So I said this came out September 20th.

00:45:30.470 --> 00:45:33.223
The next one comes out in Time Magazine,

00:45:34.470 --> 00:45:37.920
which is October 1st,
not yet their big story

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:39.970
on the same thing, but this is, again,

00:45:39.970 --> 00:45:41.790
the interference of the Russians.

00:45:41.790 --> 00:45:43.740
And then we have another story

00:45:43.740 --> 00:45:46.423
in the New York Times September 26.

00:45:47.360 --> 00:45:50.960
So I'm saying to myself,
wow, this is like a kind

00:45:50.960 --> 00:45:53.393
of a salvo cannons going.

00:45:54.290 --> 00:45:57.073
And I have to ask myself,
is there a reason for this?

00:45:58.300 --> 00:46:01.870
And I would ask you, you know, why now?

00:46:01.870 --> 00:46:05.040
Does this have to do with
the midterm elections?

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:06.653
I think it probably does.

00:46:07.540 --> 00:46:09.380
Now, did the Russians interfere?

00:46:09.380 --> 00:46:10.680
I think they probably did.

00:46:12.170 --> 00:46:13.470
I think they probably did.

00:46:16.300 --> 00:46:17.433
Was that effective?

00:46:18.650 --> 00:46:22.283
I doubt it, I very much doubt it.

00:46:23.240 --> 00:46:28.190
They didn't spend a whole
lot of money, about $100,000,

00:46:28.190 --> 00:46:31.083
which is really nothing when
you talk about the election.

00:46:32.360 --> 00:46:35.513
But they did, you know, some
interesting stuff, if you will.

00:46:37.670 --> 00:46:40.680
I believe that, to
actually think that Trump

00:46:40.680 --> 00:46:44.110
won the election because
of Russian interference,

00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:46.200
you have to be very naive for that.

00:46:46.200 --> 00:46:48.020
I mean, a farmer in Idaho

00:46:49.030 --> 00:46:51.933
was influenced by Russian propaganda?

00:46:53.370 --> 00:46:56.763
I mean, I worked in Soviet
propaganda for many years.

00:46:58.570 --> 00:47:01.873
And I'm not of the highest,
how should I say this?

00:47:02.926 --> 00:47:04.896
I mean, it's not that great propaganda.

00:47:04.896 --> 00:47:08.860
(audience laughing)
You know.

00:47:08.860 --> 00:47:10.880
It's not something I'm
proud of what I did.

00:47:10.880 --> 00:47:13.060
I mean, perhaps what I do today

00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:15.000
is because of what I did back then,

00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:16.300
you know, try and make up.

00:47:17.680 --> 00:47:22.680
But really, was Putin the
man who said, let's do this?

00:47:25.210 --> 00:47:28.530
I don't know, I can't
say yes or no to that.

00:47:28.530 --> 00:47:30.820
I'm a journalist, give me proof.

00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:35.240
Give me proof and I'll say
yes or no, depending on that.

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:37.650
But I think yeah, there
was probably an attempt

00:47:37.650 --> 00:47:39.933
to do it and so what?

00:47:41.140 --> 00:47:44.271
Does America ever interfere
in elections, anywhere?

00:47:44.271 --> 00:47:45.510
(audience laughing)

00:47:45.510 --> 00:47:46.343
Never!

00:47:47.360 --> 00:47:49.810
You know, why is it okay for you

00:47:49.810 --> 00:47:51.113
and not okay for them?

00:47:52.760 --> 00:47:54.490
You know that's a question.

00:47:54.490 --> 00:47:57.210
Well, I got an answer to that from one

00:47:57.210 --> 00:48:01.757
former CIA high ranking gentleman.

00:48:01.757 --> 00:48:04.947
"He said, well, you see,
yes, we do interfere.

00:48:04.947 --> 00:48:08.360
"But we interfere for good,
and you interfere with that."

00:48:08.360 --> 00:48:10.690
So I thought, well, that's it.

00:48:10.690 --> 00:48:12.720
So that's how it answer your question.

00:48:12.720 --> 00:48:17.560
I would say, yes, I would
not exaggerate the result

00:48:17.560 --> 00:48:19.840
of that, the impact of it.

00:48:19.840 --> 00:48:24.380
And I would say that it's being
used as a political toy ploy

00:48:24.380 --> 00:48:28.130
in this country now for
a variety of reasons.

00:48:28.130 --> 00:48:31.653
And it only really surfaced
after Hillary lost.

00:48:32.710 --> 00:48:35.560
Before that, it wasn't there.

00:48:35.560 --> 00:48:38.640
But you had to find a reason why she lost.

00:48:38.640 --> 00:48:41.500
And of course, it was
the big bad Russian bear.

00:48:41.500 --> 00:48:43.643
And that's part of the course, isn't it?

00:48:45.360 --> 00:48:47.210
Le's hear the next question.

00:48:47.210 --> 00:48:48.140
- [Man] what is your opinion

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:51.200
of the past Trump, Putin meeting?

00:48:51.200 --> 00:48:53.390
And you think it was
for show or do you think

00:48:53.390 --> 00:48:58.390
it's actually reasoning trying
to better our relations.

00:48:58.570 --> 00:49:01.513
- Even if it was for show, it was good.

00:49:02.590 --> 00:49:07.590
It was good for, how should I
put this, for public feeling.

00:49:08.890 --> 00:49:13.890
At last, these two men, one
of whom is the president

00:49:14.750 --> 00:49:16.903
of the most powerful country in the world,

00:49:17.770 --> 00:49:20.350
and the other is the
president of a country

00:49:20.350 --> 00:49:23.060
that has 10,000 nuclear warheads,

00:49:23.060 --> 00:49:26.340
and may not be that powerful,
but it's very dangerous.

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:30.130
These two men have gotten together.

00:49:30.130 --> 00:49:31.763
And that in itself is positive.

00:49:36.220 --> 00:49:37.753
Nothing was really achieved.

00:49:40.550 --> 00:49:43.810
But I would say that one
of the important thing

00:49:43.810 --> 00:49:48.493
is it seems to me that the two
men kind of like each other.

00:49:50.480 --> 00:49:51.890
And that's very important.

00:49:51.890 --> 00:49:54.160
You know, Gorbachev and Reagan,

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:58.670
were completely different human
beings, I mean completely.

00:49:58.670 --> 00:50:01.333
But they like each other.

00:50:02.220 --> 00:50:05.043
And they achieve some really
incredible breakthroughs

00:50:05.043 --> 00:50:07.043
when you think about it.

00:50:07.884 --> 00:50:09.140
So I think it was a good thing.

00:50:09.140 --> 00:50:11.270
I think it was a first step.

00:50:11.270 --> 00:50:16.270
Of course here, the reaction
to it was very negative.

00:50:18.130 --> 00:50:21.860
Because supposedly, well,
you know what Trump said

00:50:21.860 --> 00:50:26.860
that he trusted Putin, and that
he trusted his intelligence.

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:30.760
So how can you trust both?

00:50:30.760 --> 00:50:32.873
They're saying opposite things.

00:50:37.650 --> 00:50:40.100
And there was a lot of talk about the fact

00:50:40.100 --> 00:50:40.990
that he lost to Putin.

00:50:40.990 --> 00:50:41.973
What did he lose?

00:50:43.620 --> 00:50:45.100
He gave Putin the opportunity

00:50:45.100 --> 00:50:46.650
to be on the same stage with him.

00:50:46.650 --> 00:50:49.020
That's true, that's true.

00:50:49.020 --> 00:50:51.520
Did that increase Putin's stature?

00:50:51.520 --> 00:50:54.503
I don't think so, I don't think so at all.

00:50:57.120 --> 00:50:59.830
And I hope that there's a next meeting,

00:50:59.830 --> 00:51:00.883
I hope there is.

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:06.780
I hope that somehow
there will be an attempt

00:51:08.326 --> 00:51:12.150
to break out of the
situation that we're in now.

00:51:12.150 --> 00:51:16.880
I mean, I'm not again, I'm
not a big fan of Mr. Trump's.

00:51:16.880 --> 00:51:20.244
But even if he tries to do
something, the Congress,

00:51:20.244 --> 00:51:23.893
the U.S. Congress, seems hell
bent on not allowing that.

00:51:27.590 --> 00:51:29.370
And I don't know why.

00:51:29.370 --> 00:51:33.410
Now, if, if the American establishment

00:51:34.320 --> 00:51:38.097
wants regime change in
Russia, wants Putin out

00:51:41.820 --> 00:51:46.570
and someone different
in, if that is the aim

00:51:47.890 --> 00:51:52.890
of the people who run this
country, let's put it that way,

00:51:57.263 --> 00:52:01.290
that is extreme dangerous.

00:52:01.290 --> 00:52:02.540
It's not going to happen.

00:52:03.500 --> 00:52:04.750
It's not going to happen.

00:52:06.240 --> 00:52:08.060
Russians are living worse today

00:52:08.060 --> 00:52:10.350
than they did five years ago.

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:11.650
The sanctions are hurting.

00:52:13.130 --> 00:52:15.173
But the average Russian is saying,

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:18.693
we're being punished
for things we never did.

00:52:20.230 --> 00:52:21.653
This is not fair.

00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:26.803
This is the American bully,
throwing his weight around.

00:52:28.280 --> 00:52:31.153
And we'll take it, we can take it.

00:52:32.450 --> 00:52:33.350
And I think that's true.

00:52:33.350 --> 00:52:36.913
The Russians, the tougher it
is, the more tough they become.

00:52:37.830 --> 00:52:40.610
They're pretty weak when things are good.

00:52:40.610 --> 00:52:43.340
No, it's true, but when things are tough,

00:52:43.340 --> 00:52:45.750
that's when they're really, you know.

00:52:45.750 --> 00:52:50.750
And I was reading this book
by Arthur Schlesinger Jr.

00:52:53.370 --> 00:52:56.160
It's called "The Cycles
of American History,"

00:52:56.160 --> 00:53:00.710
and he dwells on 1850 when the, you know,

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:03.941
there were revolutions in Europe in 1848.

00:53:03.941 --> 00:53:05.690
And there was a Hungarian Revolution.

00:53:05.690 --> 00:53:09.930
And the leader of that revolution
was a man called Kossuth.

00:53:09.930 --> 00:53:14.050
And finally, that revolution
was, and he was destroyed

00:53:14.050 --> 00:53:16.163
by the Austrians and the Russians.

00:53:17.540 --> 00:53:21.330
And so speaking in Congress,
an American senator

00:53:27.630 --> 00:53:32.210
said that the United
States should interfere.

00:53:32.210 --> 00:53:33.533
This is 1850.

00:53:35.770 --> 00:53:40.770
And Senator Hale, and
there's this quote, he said,

00:53:44.100 --> 00:53:47.920
the future historian might
start off his chapter

00:53:47.920 --> 00:53:50.323
about the year 1850 in
the following manner.

00:53:51.470 --> 00:53:55.520
At the commencement of this
year, the American Senate,

00:53:55.520 --> 00:53:58.847
the highest legislative body of the world,

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:03.980
the world wisest, greatest
and most magnanimous people

00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:06.813
that ever lived or ever will live,

00:54:08.500 --> 00:54:12.100
forgetting and neglecting
the trifling local affairs,

00:54:12.100 --> 00:54:14.380
which considered their own limits,

00:54:14.380 --> 00:54:18.210
constituted themselves into
a high court and proceeded

00:54:18.210 --> 00:54:20.850
to try the nations of the earth

00:54:20.850 --> 00:54:23.193
for atrocious acts of despotism.

00:54:25.170 --> 00:54:29.250
1850, the idea that the
United States has the right

00:54:30.970 --> 00:54:34.520
to interfere in other countries
because they behaved badly

00:54:34.520 --> 00:54:35.930
from a U.S. or other point of view,

00:54:35.930 --> 00:54:37.770
that was from a U.S. point of view,

00:54:37.770 --> 00:54:41.320
1850, well, then you begin to understand

00:54:41.320 --> 00:54:42.810
a lot of other things,

00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:44.400
because that's what the United States

00:54:44.400 --> 00:54:46.110
has done time and time again,

00:54:46.110 --> 00:54:50.250
but when another country does
that, that's not permissible.

00:54:50.250 --> 00:54:55.230
So in Syria, if the U.S. goes
in to protect the so called

00:54:55.230 --> 00:55:00.230
and support, the so called
moderate opposition.

00:55:00.230 --> 00:55:04.920
And the Russians go in to
support Saddam, not Saddam

00:55:06.616 --> 00:55:07.741
- [Audience] Assad.

00:55:07.741 --> 00:55:11.590
- Assad, the Americans
can do it, that's okay.

00:55:11.590 --> 00:55:15.570
The Russians can't, you know,
where's your logic here?

00:55:15.570 --> 00:55:18.000
It's a tit-for-tat thing, isn't it?

00:55:18.000 --> 00:55:21.800
So, you know, that's
pretty much in my view

00:55:21.800 --> 00:55:23.160
the answer there.

00:55:23.160 --> 00:55:25.113
That's how I look at this issue.

00:55:29.340 --> 00:55:30.173
- Is there someone over on this side?

00:55:30.173 --> 00:55:31.560
- Are we done?

00:55:31.560 --> 00:55:32.570
- No, no, pick on this side.

00:55:32.570 --> 00:55:34.063
There's a question right over here.

00:55:38.348 --> 00:55:39.990
Perhaps, if you would
also identify yourselves

00:55:39.990 --> 00:55:41.500
before you ask your question.

00:55:41.500 --> 00:55:43.070
- [Rob] Okay, my name is Rob Foreman,

00:55:43.070 --> 00:55:45.220
I work for the medical school here.

00:55:45.220 --> 00:55:48.857
I used to listen to you on
radio Moscow so (laughs).

00:55:49.765 --> 00:55:51.800
- That was a long time ago.

00:55:51.800 --> 00:55:53.070
- [Rob] See the gray hairs?

00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:54.486
- Yeah, well--

00:55:54.486 --> 00:55:56.560
(audience laughing)

00:55:56.560 --> 00:56:00.053
- [Rob] But I'd like to frame
it a little differently.

00:56:00.960 --> 00:56:05.860
That I'm not so sure I
perceive Americans to be hating

00:56:05.860 --> 00:56:08.710
what Russia did in interfering,

00:56:08.710 --> 00:56:10.810
although America has some of that attitude

00:56:10.810 --> 00:56:15.810
that you described, but
disliking the fact that someone

00:56:17.530 --> 00:56:22.290
who is largely considered to
be an incompetent president,

00:56:22.290 --> 00:56:25.770
who does not have the interests
of this country at heart

00:56:27.410 --> 00:56:32.390
is beholden to the Russians,
even if the Russians,

00:56:32.390 --> 00:56:35.110
what they did was comparable
to the what the Americans

00:56:35.110 --> 00:56:38.260
have done in Central America
and who knows where else.

00:56:38.260 --> 00:56:41.070
And on that basis,

00:56:41.070 --> 00:56:45.260
I guess my question is A,
do you think that analysis

00:56:45.260 --> 00:56:47.480
is accurate and B, why not?

00:56:47.480 --> 00:56:52.480
If not, because I don't know
that it is so much an attack

00:56:52.630 --> 00:56:57.630
on what the Russians did
in 2016 as on the person

00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:02.003
who seems to be in the
pocket of those who did it?

00:57:03.770 --> 00:57:08.770
- Well, I would say this, it
was Kennedy who used to say,

00:57:08.900 --> 00:57:10.363
I'll say this to that, right.

00:57:12.350 --> 00:57:13.483
I will say this.

00:57:19.797 --> 00:57:24.410
What I said initially,
when I compared what Trump

00:57:24.410 --> 00:57:27.540
was saying during the
presidential campaign

00:57:27.540 --> 00:57:29.770
and what Hillary was saying,

00:57:29.770 --> 00:57:32.470
it's obvious to anyone that the Russians

00:57:32.470 --> 00:57:36.860
would support Trump, it's obvious.

00:57:36.860 --> 00:57:39.160
And Americans should understand that.

00:57:39.160 --> 00:57:41.040
Not because of what Trump stands for,

00:57:41.040 --> 00:57:45.950
not because he's a whatever
he is, not because he wants

00:57:45.950 --> 00:57:47.920
the Supreme Court to be more conservative.

00:57:47.920 --> 00:57:49.080
Russians don't care about that.

00:57:49.080 --> 00:57:51.970
They don't even, it's not
part of what they think.

00:57:51.970 --> 00:57:55.160
But they think about the
relationship between Russia

00:57:55.160 --> 00:57:56.740
and the United States.

00:57:56.740 --> 00:57:59.320
And judging from what he said, back then,

00:57:59.320 --> 00:58:01.743
their relationship could improve.

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:05.420
And that's basically it on the one hand.

00:58:05.420 --> 00:58:08.640
On the other hand, you
recall as well as I do,

00:58:08.640 --> 00:58:11.003
how many American senators,

00:58:14.410 --> 00:58:17.650
how many political figures said

00:58:17.650 --> 00:58:20.650
that the Russians were trying
to destroy American democracy.

00:58:22.120 --> 00:58:23.830
What does that mean?

00:58:23.830 --> 00:58:28.430
How so that the aim of the Russians

00:58:28.430 --> 00:58:32.053
was to somehow destroy American democracy?

00:58:33.560 --> 00:58:35.280
There's none of that.

00:58:35.280 --> 00:58:37.570
So I can understand what you're saying.

00:58:37.570 --> 00:58:39.600
But it really is not at all.

00:58:39.600 --> 00:58:44.240
It doesn't correspond to what Mr. Putin

00:58:44.240 --> 00:58:47.510
or the majority of people in
Russia really cared about,

00:58:47.510 --> 00:58:48.900
or care about today.

00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:51.610
Today, there is much less support

00:58:51.610 --> 00:58:54.360
for Donald Trump in Russia than there was.

00:58:54.360 --> 00:58:57.450
People are asking themselves,
who the hell is this man?

00:58:57.450 --> 00:58:59.410
He comes here and he says
one thing and he goes away

00:58:59.410 --> 00:59:01.410
and says something completely different.

00:59:02.290 --> 00:59:05.360
You know, if today he says,
yes, tomorrow he says no

00:59:05.360 --> 00:59:06.930
to the same thing.

00:59:06.930 --> 00:59:09.010
So there are lots of questions.

00:59:09.010 --> 00:59:13.149
And there's kind of a,

00:59:13.149 --> 00:59:14.230
how should I put this?

00:59:14.230 --> 00:59:16.810
They don't take him too seriously anymore,

00:59:16.810 --> 00:59:19.753
except that he has enormous power.

00:59:23.970 --> 00:59:26.020
- [Alexander] My name is
Alexander, I'm an undergraduate.

00:59:26.020 --> 00:59:27.850
You touched on at the
beginning of your talk

00:59:27.850 --> 00:59:31.710
about how there's intense
American hatred in Russia.

00:59:31.710 --> 00:59:33.990
And just now you say that,
you know, most Russians

00:59:33.990 --> 00:59:35.810
would want to improve relations.

00:59:35.810 --> 00:59:39.370
Why do you think, how does
that help push into have like,

00:59:39.370 --> 00:59:42.870
very bad relations with
America or like a bad image

00:59:42.870 --> 00:59:44.910
of Americans and Russian minds?

00:59:44.910 --> 00:59:48.080
And also, do you think that
this hatred is reciprocal,

00:59:48.080 --> 00:59:50.010
like, is there a distrust of

00:59:50.010 --> 00:59:51.773
or dislike of Russians in America?

00:59:53.700 --> 00:59:55.750
- I think the word hatred is a bit strong

00:59:56.990 --> 00:59:58.063
and I didn't use it.

00:59:59.010 --> 01:00:02.940
I said that the attitude of Russians today

01:00:02.940 --> 01:00:05.430
towards American is more negative

01:00:05.430 --> 01:00:08.890
than it was back during the
Cold War, the worst times,

01:00:08.890 --> 01:00:11.160
and I think it's mutual.

01:00:11.160 --> 01:00:16.160
And I think that that's the
result of what the politicians

01:00:18.450 --> 01:00:21.410
have said, what they've
done and what the media

01:00:21.410 --> 01:00:23.223
is doing in both countries.

01:00:24.070 --> 01:00:27.210
And I think it's an
issue of political will.

01:00:27.210 --> 01:00:30.500
Because before Gorbachev
came to power in Russia,

01:00:30.500 --> 01:00:34.930
in Soviet Union, excuse me,
the attitude was negative

01:00:34.930 --> 01:00:37.940
in this country, very
negative towards Soviet Union,

01:00:37.940 --> 01:00:39.770
and the Russians, you know, the Russians,

01:00:39.770 --> 01:00:41.507
oh, the Russians are coming (mumbles).

01:00:42.920 --> 01:00:45.743
But as soon as Gorbachev
started changing things,

01:00:46.920 --> 01:00:51.613
glasnost, perestroika,
suddenly became Gorby,

01:00:52.480 --> 01:00:57.170
he became, you know,
America's favorite guy.

01:00:57.170 --> 01:01:01.360
So I think that if there
is a desire on both sides

01:01:05.280 --> 01:01:09.100
to change that attitude, it
can be done very quickly.

01:01:09.100 --> 01:01:11.300
And that's why I say
that we're manipulated.

01:01:12.190 --> 01:01:13.197
We are manipulated.

01:01:13.197 --> 01:01:15.497
And we all say, well,
I'm totally independent.

01:01:16.520 --> 01:01:21.520
It's not true, we make our decisions,

01:01:22.800 --> 01:01:27.520
and we come to certain conclusions
because of what we read,

01:01:27.520 --> 01:01:30.773
because of what we see and
because of what we hear.

01:01:32.330 --> 01:01:34.793
So, basically, that's it.

01:01:36.170 --> 01:01:39.270
I would say that certainly
the internet allows us

01:01:39.270 --> 01:01:41.990
to get a much broader picture.

01:01:41.990 --> 01:01:44.110
In fact, we could communicate

01:01:44.110 --> 01:01:46.483
with the other side via the internet.

01:01:47.520 --> 01:01:50.900
It's not happening very
much, but it is a little bit.

01:01:50.900 --> 01:01:55.050
So that the, how should I put
this, the ordinary citizen

01:01:55.990 --> 01:02:00.990
could do a lot to change what's
happening in both countries,

01:02:01.400 --> 01:02:02.773
and it's a two way street.

01:02:03.903 --> 01:02:05.770
And I think it's people like you,

01:02:05.770 --> 01:02:07.423
that is to say of your age,

01:02:09.130 --> 01:02:12.090
they're the ones who for me,
are the reason for optimism

01:02:12.950 --> 01:02:14.800
because you can do this.

01:02:14.800 --> 01:02:19.053
Whereas people of my age
and slightly younger,

01:02:21.340 --> 01:02:22.493
can do far less.

01:02:23.350 --> 01:02:27.110
So I would hope that, you
know, what I've said today

01:02:30.700 --> 01:02:33.950
might lead you to look into this.

01:02:33.950 --> 01:02:35.850
And I don't say to anyone, believe me.

01:02:37.050 --> 01:02:39.973
Trust me, heaven forbid, look into it.

01:02:41.000 --> 01:02:42.150
That's all I would say.

01:02:43.000 --> 01:02:44.960
- So I was struck in both your talking

01:02:44.960 --> 01:02:48.760
and now your sort of mutual
indictment of the media

01:02:48.760 --> 01:02:51.830
on both sides and I've
been curious for a while

01:02:51.830 --> 01:02:56.010
about how this may or may not be different

01:02:56.010 --> 01:03:01.010
to what this picture looks
like say in the mid 1980s.

01:03:02.408 --> 01:03:03.740
- I wonder if you could recall,

01:03:03.740 --> 01:03:05.400
so just take the New York Times.

01:03:05.400 --> 01:03:07.730
I mean, I wonder if you
could recall with you know,

01:03:07.730 --> 01:03:10.540
what New York Times reporting about Russia

01:03:10.540 --> 01:03:13.120
about the Soviet Union
was like, in the 80s.

01:03:13.120 --> 01:03:13.953
- Yeah.

01:03:13.953 --> 01:03:16.370
- And whether you see a
significant difference--

01:03:16.370 --> 01:03:17.820
- Let me give you an example.

01:03:20.620 --> 01:03:23.687
Back in the 1970s, 1980s,

01:03:25.310 --> 01:03:27.830
all of the major television networks,

01:03:27.830 --> 01:03:31.360
which are the most powerful
in the sense of affecting

01:03:31.360 --> 01:03:34.253
back then especially
affecting people's use.

01:03:35.160 --> 01:03:38.100
All three of the major networks, ABC, CBS,

01:03:38.100 --> 01:03:43.053
and NBC had bureaus in Moscow.

01:03:44.530 --> 01:03:47.400
The head of the ABC Bureau was a woman

01:03:47.400 --> 01:03:48.800
by the name of Anne Garrels.

01:03:50.290 --> 01:03:53.170
Well, that was a rare thing
in those years for a bureau

01:03:53.170 --> 01:03:55.340
to have a woman as a chief.

01:03:55.340 --> 01:03:57.870
Not only that, but she spoke Russian

01:03:57.870 --> 01:03:58.703
and I'd like to tell you

01:03:58.703 --> 01:04:00.890
that the vast majority
of American journalists

01:04:00.890 --> 01:04:05.033
did not speak Russian, they
had interpreters, she didn't.

01:04:06.520 --> 01:04:09.493
And one day when, and I
met her at some reception,

01:04:10.540 --> 01:04:13.370
and I was doing the show that, you know,

01:04:13.370 --> 01:04:14.823
I did for Moscow radio.

01:04:16.820 --> 01:04:18.567
And we talked and she said,

01:04:18.567 --> 01:04:20.677
"You should be on U.S. television."

01:04:22.160 --> 01:04:24.483
I said, well, you know,
how does that happen?

01:04:25.480 --> 01:04:26.990
She called up Ted Koppel,

01:04:26.990 --> 01:04:29.793
you remember who he was right, Nightline?

01:04:31.400 --> 01:04:33.123
I hope you've not forgotten.

01:04:33.123 --> 01:04:34.250
(audience laughing)

01:04:34.250 --> 01:04:36.130
And she said, you know,
Ted, instead of having

01:04:36.130 --> 01:04:38.470
dissidents on all the time,

01:04:38.470 --> 01:04:43.000
who represent 0.0 point something

01:04:43.000 --> 01:04:46.740
of the country of the Soviet Union,

01:04:46.740 --> 01:04:49.700
she's called on someone
who can can express

01:04:49.700 --> 01:04:52.573
the Soviet viewpoint and
who can do it in English.

01:04:53.730 --> 01:04:58.730
So I got on Nightline, and since I was not

01:04:58.940 --> 01:05:02.990
your typical Russian as far
as the American audience

01:05:02.990 --> 01:05:04.683
was concerned, who is this?

01:05:06.572 --> 01:05:09.320
I had great ratings and so I was on again

01:05:09.320 --> 01:05:11.010
and again and again.

01:05:11.010 --> 01:05:13.903
And then on NBC and
CBS and here and there.

01:05:14.960 --> 01:05:16.963
Today, that wouldn't happen.

01:05:18.460 --> 01:05:23.460
I was allowed to argue
the Soviet viewpoint

01:05:24.760 --> 01:05:25.993
on American television.

01:05:27.880 --> 01:05:29.173
I was allowed to do that.

01:05:30.470 --> 01:05:32.500
Ted would invite someone
from the State Department

01:05:32.500 --> 01:05:35.570
to rebut me and very often the
State Department would say,

01:05:35.570 --> 01:05:36.740
we're not gonna do this 'cause Constantine

01:05:36.740 --> 01:05:38.880
is a pain in the what sits

01:05:38.880 --> 01:05:41.553
and we don't wanna talk
to him, that's fine.

01:05:43.190 --> 01:05:46.940
Today, I would not get that possibility

01:05:46.940 --> 01:05:50.033
to be on a major network regularly.

01:05:51.400 --> 01:05:52.700
And that's what's changed.

01:05:53.640 --> 01:05:55.600
That's what's really changed.

01:05:55.600 --> 01:06:00.600
And I've come to think that
Government censorship official

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:05.650
or not official, and so
Soviet Union was official.

01:06:05.650 --> 01:06:08.560
It was an official censor,
you have to go with your piece

01:06:08.560 --> 01:06:12.520
and the center would read
it, stamp it, censorship.

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:14.640
Today that doesn't exist in Russia.

01:06:14.640 --> 01:06:17.551
But of course there is
censorship when, you know,

01:06:17.551 --> 01:06:20.060
the boss says no, you can't say that

01:06:20.060 --> 01:06:21.260
or you can't print that.

01:06:22.100 --> 01:06:25.700
So, and I'm talking about
mainstream media, right?

01:06:25.700 --> 01:06:29.323
So I call that government censorship.

01:06:30.480 --> 01:06:33.640
But there's something that
I call company censorship.

01:06:40.970 --> 01:06:42.587
And it's just as effective.

01:06:43.440 --> 01:06:46.780
And what's happened with
American media much of my regret,

01:06:46.780 --> 01:06:49.520
is that what used to be independent,

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:54.443
you know, CNN belonged to
Ted Turner and he ran it.

01:06:55.490 --> 01:07:00.233
CBS was created by William
Paley and he ran it.

01:07:01.390 --> 01:07:06.390
But gradually, many of
these very important sources

01:07:06.450 --> 01:07:09.640
were bought by larger companies.

01:07:09.640 --> 01:07:14.640
CNN, he sold it NBC, NBC was
bought by AOL, ABC by Disney.

01:07:17.810 --> 01:07:22.810
And so these very important
media, whatever organizations

01:07:24.410 --> 01:07:29.410
became part of huge
conglomerates and information

01:07:29.770 --> 01:07:31.840
was then made very different.

01:07:31.840 --> 01:07:35.130
The attitude towards what media

01:07:35.130 --> 01:07:38.233
is supposed to do changed dramatically.

01:07:39.420 --> 01:07:44.150
I asked to the group of
people, I paid for it actually,

01:07:44.150 --> 01:07:46.350
just try to find in the New York Times

01:07:47.230 --> 01:07:50.187
over the past three years, 2015, '16, '17,

01:07:53.270 --> 01:07:56.283
any positive article about Russia,

01:07:57.940 --> 01:08:00.993
one positive article, didn't find any.

01:08:02.580 --> 01:08:06.823
Not one, now I say myself,
is that isn't censorship,

01:08:07.850 --> 01:08:09.350
then what is it?

01:08:09.350 --> 01:08:13.840
I mean, there have to be people
who come to Russia and say,

01:08:13.840 --> 01:08:16.563
gee, they have good ice cream, you know,

01:08:17.420 --> 01:08:20.970
or they have great theater, and they do.

01:08:20.970 --> 01:08:24.400
And people are standing in
line for theater tickets.

01:08:24.400 --> 01:08:26.593
And that's a nice thing, right?

01:08:27.510 --> 01:08:32.510
Give a more or less accurate,
but no, it's all negative.

01:08:33.840 --> 01:08:38.840
And that's the New York Times,
let alone everything else.

01:08:41.340 --> 01:08:44.140
And it's to me, it's
terribly disappointing,

01:08:44.140 --> 01:08:48.250
because I was, you know, I
worked here for many CNBC

01:08:48.250 --> 01:08:50.350
for quite a few years until a man

01:08:50.350 --> 01:08:52.770
by the name of Roger Ailes,

01:08:52.770 --> 01:08:55.440
I don't know if you recall who that was,

01:08:55.440 --> 01:08:59.943
he killed our program
because we were too liberal.

01:09:01.220 --> 01:09:04.420
Just didn't resume the
contract, and that was that.

01:09:04.420 --> 01:09:07.410
So I know what freedom
to press is on both sides

01:09:07.410 --> 01:09:09.320
of the fence as it were.

01:09:09.320 --> 01:09:10.980
It's a different issue.

01:09:10.980 --> 01:09:13.890
So that's what I'm saying
that we in a strange way

01:09:13.890 --> 01:09:18.890
corporate censorship is just
as effective and sometimes

01:09:19.360 --> 01:09:23.163
far more sophisticated
than government censorship.

01:09:24.510 --> 01:09:25.840
- I think we're over on
this side of the room

01:09:25.840 --> 01:09:27.610
and people in the middle are also welcome

01:09:27.610 --> 01:09:30.370
to ask questions somewhere.

01:09:30.370 --> 01:09:32.690
We see a head way in the back.

01:09:32.690 --> 01:09:34.713
- Actually, you could lob the microphone.

01:09:42.360 --> 01:09:44.380
- [Rick] So, my name is Rick Schneider.

01:09:44.380 --> 01:09:46.310
I work with Rivendell Institute at Yale.

01:09:46.310 --> 01:09:47.143
- Where are you?

01:09:47.143 --> 01:09:47.976
- [Rick] Right here.

01:09:47.976 --> 01:09:48.820
- Okay, thank you.

01:09:48.820 --> 01:09:51.880
- [Rick] I'm also a visiting
professor, have been at GMO

01:09:51.880 --> 01:09:53.650
and currently at Vushka.

01:09:53.650 --> 01:09:54.623
- Yeah.

01:09:54.623 --> 01:09:58.460
- [Rick] So my question
is, from your discussions,

01:09:58.460 --> 01:10:02.200
experience with leaders in Russia,

01:10:02.200 --> 01:10:05.380
do you sense any room for
compromise on Ukraine?

01:10:05.380 --> 01:10:07.500
I mean, after all the, maybe the big

01:10:07.500 --> 01:10:09.260
sticking point right now in our relations

01:10:09.260 --> 01:10:12.040
is all of those sanctions,
most of the sanctions

01:10:12.040 --> 01:10:13.270
other than the Magnitsky, of course,

01:10:13.270 --> 01:10:15.500
but most of the sanctions
are about Ukraine

01:10:15.500 --> 01:10:17.730
and Donbass is a big problem.

01:10:17.730 --> 01:10:20.580
So, is there room for
compromise to fix that?

01:10:20.580 --> 01:10:23.470
- I bet, first of all,
I applaud the question

01:10:23.470 --> 01:10:25.333
because the key word is compromise.

01:10:26.270 --> 01:10:27.653
The key word is compromise.

01:10:29.280 --> 01:10:33.103
I had asked for the
possibility to show a map.

01:10:34.340 --> 01:10:37.793
Is that still possible or is
that not possible anymore?

01:10:40.310 --> 01:10:41.760
I wanna answer your question,

01:10:43.250 --> 01:10:45.350
because I wanna show something

01:10:45.350 --> 01:10:46.183
that I think is important.

01:10:46.183 --> 01:10:49.084
And I asked, oh my!

01:10:49.084 --> 01:10:52.084
(audience laughing)

01:10:56.180 --> 01:11:01.180
I asked that a map of Russia with Ukraine

01:11:03.150 --> 01:11:05.130
be put up on the screen and hopefully--

01:11:05.130 --> 01:11:06.078
- [Technician] This will take,

01:11:06.078 --> 01:11:07.917
it'll take a couple of seconds.

01:11:07.917 --> 01:11:11.817
- I'm in no hurry, and a
map of the United States

01:11:11.817 --> 01:11:15.383
and Mexico be put up on screen.

01:11:17.410 --> 01:11:18.243
Now,

01:11:24.880 --> 01:11:28.563
let me go back to the
Cuban Missile Crisis, 1962.

01:11:32.960 --> 01:11:37.960
Two totally independent
leaders, Nikita Khrushchev

01:11:41.366 --> 01:11:45.120
and Fidel Castro decide
that it would be a good idea

01:11:47.140 --> 01:11:50.523
for Russia to deploy
missiles on Cuban soil.

01:11:52.010 --> 01:11:54.420
Did they have the right
to make that decision?

01:11:54.420 --> 01:11:57.670
Absolutely, two independent countries,

01:11:57.670 --> 01:12:01.867
and Fidel Castro was not
a satellite, for sure.

01:12:04.970 --> 01:12:08.180
So, is that, okay.

01:12:08.180 --> 01:12:10.670
So they make that agreement.

01:12:10.670 --> 01:12:13.980
And so it's begin bringing
in them the missiles

01:12:13.980 --> 01:12:15.230
or the parts to assemble.

01:12:16.360 --> 01:12:18.803
And the United States discovers this.

01:12:20.350 --> 01:12:21.780
- [Technician] That's not the (mumbles).

01:12:25.197 --> 01:12:26.030
No, no, no.

01:12:26.030 --> 01:12:30.820
- And Kennedy says to the Russians,

01:12:32.770 --> 01:12:36.283
turn your ships around,
or we will sink them.

01:12:37.830 --> 01:12:40.587
And if there leads to
World War III, so be it.

01:12:45.770 --> 01:12:49.163
And the Russians turn their ships around.

01:12:50.940 --> 01:12:52.293
But there was a compromise.

01:12:53.970 --> 01:12:56.563
Not made public in this
country at that time.

01:12:58.160 --> 01:13:02.210
Kennedy agreed to pull
out American missiles

01:13:02.210 --> 01:13:06.000
that were deployed in Turkey in exchange,

01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:09.320
because the Russian said,
look, you have missiles

01:13:09.320 --> 01:13:11.283
in Turkey right on our border, almost.

01:13:13.080 --> 01:13:15.330
You say our missiles are
an existential threat,

01:13:15.330 --> 01:13:17.170
but so are yours.

01:13:17.170 --> 01:13:19.713
Take them out and we will not deploy ours.

01:13:20.640 --> 01:13:22.320
That was a compromise.

01:13:22.320 --> 01:13:25.210
Now, Kennedy asked that
this not be made public

01:13:25.210 --> 01:13:28.050
because it would kind of
be seen as a loss of faith

01:13:28.050 --> 01:13:30.300
and blah, blah, blah,
and it wasn't made public

01:13:30.300 --> 01:13:32.150
and then it was later.

01:13:32.150 --> 01:13:34.393
So that was a compromise.

01:13:35.560 --> 01:13:40.070
And it helps avoid World War III.

01:13:40.070 --> 01:13:41.670
Well, this is not yet happening--

01:13:41.670 --> 01:13:43.490
- You may have to sort of ask folks

01:13:43.490 --> 01:13:45.610
to conjure in their minds.

01:13:45.610 --> 01:13:47.280
- All right, I will do that.

01:13:47.280 --> 01:13:49.440
- Yes, I think you wanted to--

01:13:49.440 --> 01:13:54.440
- So now the Russian leadership sees NATO

01:13:56.070 --> 01:13:57.623
as an existential threat.

01:13:59.140 --> 01:14:02.110
Rightly or wrongly, that's not the point.

01:14:02.110 --> 01:14:04.710
The point is that that's
the way it's seen.

01:14:04.710 --> 01:14:06.430
And this is not politics.

01:14:06.430 --> 01:14:08.870
You know, why would you bring NATO

01:14:08.870 --> 01:14:12.450
closer and closer to
our borders, they say.

01:14:12.450 --> 01:14:14.900
In Latvia, it's on the border,

01:14:14.900 --> 01:14:17.223
in Estonia, it's on the border.

01:14:18.410 --> 01:14:23.410
Now, Ukraine is being, is moving westward,

01:14:27.940 --> 01:14:30.360
the complicated issue, the United States

01:14:30.360 --> 01:14:33.770
has played a certain role in
this, that's not the point.

01:14:33.770 --> 01:14:34.920
It's moving westward.

01:14:34.920 --> 01:14:39.920
If it ultimately goes
into the western fold

01:14:40.160 --> 01:14:41.660
or whatever you wanna call it,

01:14:42.630 --> 01:14:46.580
then it's logical to surmise that Ukraine

01:14:46.580 --> 01:14:51.580
will join the European Union
and will become a NATO member.

01:14:52.720 --> 01:14:57.720
Now Ukraine has a border with Russia.

01:14:59.020 --> 01:15:04.020
Not only that, but Crimea,
which was traditionally Russian,

01:15:04.620 --> 01:15:09.380
but I'm not, we can go
into the complicated thing

01:15:09.380 --> 01:15:13.170
about how Ukraine was
Russia and was (mumbles),

01:15:13.170 --> 01:15:18.170
not the point, but Sebastopol
was always the base

01:15:19.500 --> 01:15:21.423
of the Navy, the Russian Navy,

01:15:22.880 --> 01:15:24.563
naval fleet of the Black Sea.

01:15:25.800 --> 01:15:30.800
So if Crimea remains Ukraine,

01:15:30.880 --> 01:15:33.893
and if Ukraine becomes part of NATO,

01:15:35.330 --> 01:15:36.810
the Russian fleet won't be.

01:15:36.810 --> 01:15:39.540
So that's the poll, but the American 60

01:15:39.540 --> 01:15:40.790
might very well be there.

01:15:42.130 --> 01:15:45.563
And NATO will be on
Russia's southwest border.

01:15:46.570 --> 01:15:51.200
And the Russians see this
as an existential danger.

01:15:51.200 --> 01:15:54.860
And they say, we will not permit it.

01:15:54.860 --> 01:15:59.060
Now, does that correspond
to international law?

01:15:59.060 --> 01:16:00.863
No, it doesn't.

01:16:01.840 --> 01:16:03.700
When you talk about existential threat,

01:16:03.700 --> 01:16:05.920
you say, I don't care
about international law,

01:16:05.920 --> 01:16:07.563
as in the Cuban Missile Crisis.

01:16:08.740 --> 01:16:12.640
You say, no, we don't
give a damn how you feel.

01:16:12.640 --> 01:16:15.010
We're not going to allow this.

01:16:15.010 --> 01:16:18.213
It is my opinion that
had from the very outset,

01:16:20.060 --> 01:16:25.060
been some kind of internationally
negotiated agreement,

01:16:25.220 --> 01:16:27.560
that Ukraine would not
become a member of NATO

01:16:27.560 --> 01:16:29.680
for at least the next 50 years,

01:16:29.680 --> 01:16:31.673
there would be no Ukrainian problem.

01:16:35.870 --> 01:16:39.663
Why did I ask for a map of
the United States and Mexico?

01:16:41.110 --> 01:16:42.180
Well, the United States

01:16:42.180 --> 01:16:44.860
has a pretty large border with Mexico.

01:16:44.860 --> 01:16:48.560
Now imagine that you have
a revolution in Mexico.

01:16:48.560 --> 01:16:50.160
That's not that hard to imagine.

01:16:51.640 --> 01:16:54.300
And imagine that the
government that comes to power

01:16:54.300 --> 01:16:57.770
is not a big fan of the
United States of America.

01:16:57.770 --> 01:17:00.103
I think that too, is not
difficult to imagine.

01:17:01.370 --> 01:17:03.870
But since it's a little
bit afraid of big brother,

01:17:05.130 --> 01:17:10.130
it asks the Russians to
send over three or four

01:17:10.350 --> 01:17:14.683
or five divisions to be placed
on the U.S. Mexican border.

01:17:15.842 --> 01:17:17.493
You think the U.S. would accept that?

01:17:19.500 --> 01:17:23.073
So why would the Russians accept this?

01:17:25.050 --> 01:17:26.750
That's what it's really all about.

01:17:27.630 --> 01:17:28.930
Does there have to be a compromise?

01:17:28.930 --> 01:17:30.750
In my opinion, yes.

01:17:30.750 --> 01:17:35.340
And the compromise has to
be that Ukraine guarantee

01:17:35.340 --> 01:17:36.990
will not become a member of NATO.

01:17:37.950 --> 01:17:40.663
And then the compromise is
that the Russians get out.

01:17:41.860 --> 01:17:44.133
We get out, but there's the guarantee.

01:17:45.990 --> 01:17:47.563
Crimea is a different issue.

01:17:49.070 --> 01:17:52.070
There could be an agreement
that let's do another referendum

01:17:53.250 --> 01:17:55.013
under international supervision.

01:17:56.510 --> 01:17:58.460
Let's see what the people of Crimea want.

01:17:58.460 --> 01:18:00.360
Do they really wanna be part of Russia

01:18:01.240 --> 01:18:02.790
or do they wanna be part of Ukraine?

01:18:02.790 --> 01:18:04.980
Or do they wanna be an
independent republic?

01:18:04.980 --> 01:18:07.550
I know that the answer
will be overwhelming.

01:18:07.550 --> 01:18:09.480
And so does everyone.

01:18:09.480 --> 01:18:13.320
Which is why no one is even
suggesting because the majority

01:18:13.320 --> 01:18:17.490
of the people who live in
Crimea wanna be part of Russia.

01:18:17.490 --> 01:18:20.580
It's a tradition, it's existed
that way for a long time.

01:18:21.920 --> 01:18:23.420
But basically, that's my answer.

01:18:23.420 --> 01:18:25.670
Yes, you need a compromise.

01:18:25.670 --> 01:18:28.890
And if neither side is
willing to compromise,

01:18:28.890 --> 01:18:32.563
well, then we're in deep whatever.

01:18:35.330 --> 01:18:37.203
- We're over here for the next question.

01:18:39.073 --> 01:18:41.560
(man speaks in foreign language)

01:18:41.560 --> 01:18:42.410
I can't hear you.

01:18:43.304 --> 01:18:45.130
(man speaks in foreign language)

01:18:45.130 --> 01:18:45.963
You guys speak Russian?

01:18:45.963 --> 01:18:47.730
- [Man] No, I gonna switch to English.

01:18:47.730 --> 01:18:49.883
I just wanted to greet you in Russian.

01:18:50.800 --> 01:18:53.290
I've been following you
for many years, your work.

01:18:53.290 --> 01:18:56.040
Going back to tell
telebridge was Phil Donahue

01:18:56.040 --> 01:19:00.770
and certainly, some of
us remember those days

01:19:00.770 --> 01:19:02.890
in Washington back in the Soviet Union.

01:19:02.890 --> 01:19:06.213
I'm from Ukraine, just for the record.

01:19:07.755 --> 01:19:12.088
So I certainly share your
view about a lot of things

01:19:13.990 --> 01:19:16.210
that you speak about.

01:19:16.210 --> 01:19:19.660
Today, however, I'm
struggling a little bit

01:19:19.660 --> 01:19:21.633
to accept your point of view.

01:19:22.860 --> 01:19:26.410
And I can't get rid of a
feeling that it's almost

01:19:26.410 --> 01:19:30.280
like a legal defense
that is trying to explain

01:19:30.280 --> 01:19:35.280
the bad behavior of a person
by the external circumstances.

01:19:36.015 --> 01:19:40.370
I'm certainly not naive or
idealistic about the policymaking

01:19:40.370 --> 01:19:42.570
in any country, including
the United States.

01:19:43.680 --> 01:19:47.653
And certainly, I do agree
that mistakes were made.

01:19:48.530 --> 01:19:50.920
Not being an expert in this field,

01:19:50.920 --> 01:19:52.980
it's difficult for me to really know

01:19:52.980 --> 01:19:56.710
the exact chronological
sequence of the events.

01:19:56.710 --> 01:19:59.020
So it's difficult to
argue what was the cause

01:19:59.020 --> 01:20:02.790
and what was the effect
of what you're describing.

01:20:02.790 --> 01:20:05.420
However, in your presentation today,

01:20:05.420 --> 01:20:09.100
I think you certainly presented Mr. Putin

01:20:09.100 --> 01:20:13.000
as a positive peace loving person.

01:20:13.000 --> 01:20:16.120
And I'm not sure that I
agree with that assessment.

01:20:16.120 --> 01:20:18.510
- I don't know, I don't.

01:20:18.510 --> 01:20:20.470
- [Man] Well, you certainly brought up

01:20:20.470 --> 01:20:23.410
a number of effects that
presented him as somebody

01:20:23.410 --> 01:20:26.930
who was continuously on the
ongoing basis reaching out

01:20:26.930 --> 01:20:29.700
to the world, not just the United States,

01:20:29.700 --> 01:20:34.670
but the West was the
peace, mission in mind.

01:20:34.670 --> 01:20:39.520
However, his actions, not
just vis-a-vis, Crimea

01:20:39.520 --> 01:20:41.980
but overall, Eastern Ukraine.

01:20:41.980 --> 01:20:45.730
His pretty aggressive actions in Syria,

01:20:45.730 --> 01:20:49.270
certainly don't create that position.

01:20:49.270 --> 01:20:52.330
So I think there's a little
bit of a disconnect between,

01:20:52.330 --> 01:20:56.320
either that's the United
States or our policymakers

01:20:56.320 --> 01:21:00.170
made that, created that
image of Putin as somebody

01:21:00.170 --> 01:21:03.669
who is a not an easy person to work with?

01:21:03.669 --> 01:21:06.050
(audience applauding)

01:21:06.050 --> 01:21:08.810
But certainly his actions in many ways,

01:21:08.810 --> 01:21:11.420
not just his actions, but
also the aggressive tactics

01:21:11.420 --> 01:21:14.760
of the military, Russian
military, in a lot of his regions

01:21:14.760 --> 01:21:16.963
don't support that, that view.

01:21:17.930 --> 01:21:22.750
But to get to my question, I
think, while I generally if we,

01:21:22.750 --> 01:21:27.750
for discussion sake, agreed
to accept that the U.S.

01:21:27.957 --> 01:21:31.490
and maybe the Western
world contributed in a way

01:21:31.490 --> 01:21:34.253
to the image of Putin that you described.

01:21:36.780 --> 01:21:39.892
Putin is not only responsible
for the foreign policy

01:21:39.892 --> 01:21:43.410
of the Russian Federation, but
also for its domestic policy,

01:21:43.410 --> 01:21:48.120
and who contributed to his
image as the domestic leader

01:21:48.120 --> 01:21:52.080
of Russia as somebody who
is fairly authoritarian

01:21:52.080 --> 01:21:55.037
in the way he runs the country, thank you.

01:21:56.080 --> 01:21:58.086
- That wasn't a question
but I'll answer it.

01:21:58.086 --> 01:21:59.890
(audience laughing)

01:21:59.890 --> 01:22:03.670
I made a point of up to 2007.

01:22:03.670 --> 01:22:08.073
And up to 2007, Putin did
nothing internationally

01:22:10.080 --> 01:22:12.800
that would speak of an
aggression, nothing at all.

01:22:12.800 --> 01:22:15.570
It all happened after 2007.

01:22:15.570 --> 01:22:20.450
It happened in 2008 with
Georgia, with the war.

01:22:20.450 --> 01:22:22.173
Officially it wasn't
Putin, it was Vladimir,

01:22:22.173 --> 01:22:26.050
but you know, so no big difference.

01:22:26.050 --> 01:22:29.000
And then all the other things
that you're talking about,

01:22:29.000 --> 01:22:30.397
but up until 2007.

01:22:30.397 --> 01:22:35.130
And so that Munich speech when
he said, enough is enough,

01:22:35.130 --> 01:22:39.060
you have to respect us, you
have to take into consideration,

01:22:39.060 --> 01:22:42.430
our interest, the world is not uni polar,

01:22:42.430 --> 01:22:47.430
it is multipolar and we will
have to keep that in mind.

01:22:48.220 --> 01:22:52.960
Incidentally, that's why
he's so popular in Russia.

01:22:52.960 --> 01:22:57.960
Not because he contributed to
people's living much better

01:22:58.240 --> 01:22:59.880
although they did, but he was lucky

01:22:59.880 --> 01:23:01.810
because the price of oil was high

01:23:01.810 --> 01:23:04.020
and so that certainly helped.

01:23:04.020 --> 01:23:08.510
But because people saw him
as someone who stood up

01:23:08.510 --> 01:23:11.793
to the American bully, and told him off.

01:23:12.750 --> 01:23:15.540
And that's where his
popularity comes from.

01:23:15.540 --> 01:23:17.130
Russia is back.

01:23:17.130 --> 01:23:19.900
We're up from our knees,
as people would say.

01:23:19.900 --> 01:23:22.250
And they have to take
us into consideration.

01:23:22.250 --> 01:23:23.610
And we don't give a damn if you like us

01:23:23.610 --> 01:23:25.410
or you don't like us.

01:23:25.410 --> 01:23:27.900
But you know, Putin is our man.

01:23:27.900 --> 01:23:29.463
He is a true patriot.

01:23:30.340 --> 01:23:31.960
And that's where his strength lies.

01:23:31.960 --> 01:23:34.840
Now, if you talk about
his domestic policies,

01:23:34.840 --> 01:23:36.850
I would absolutely agree with you that A,

01:23:36.850 --> 01:23:38.083
he is authoritarian,

01:23:39.010 --> 01:23:41.940
B, he has pretty much stymied

01:23:41.940 --> 01:23:45.503
the development of democracy in Russia.

01:23:46.990 --> 01:23:50.370
I do not agree with basically almost

01:23:50.370 --> 01:23:55.370
all of his policies in Russia,
not all but almost all.

01:23:55.830 --> 01:23:59.730
I'm much more supportive
of his international views

01:23:59.730 --> 01:24:01.293
than with his domestic views.

01:24:02.250 --> 01:24:04.290
So I have no argument with you.

01:24:04.290 --> 01:24:08.750
I did not present, I don't
think I did present him as a,

01:24:08.750 --> 01:24:12.670
you know, a lover of peace.

01:24:12.670 --> 01:24:16.110
I was simply making the point
that up until a certain moment

01:24:18.350 --> 01:24:20.553
you could not really
blame him for anything.

01:24:22.090 --> 01:24:26.100
And that well, I say find it,
just point me, you know, say,

01:24:26.100 --> 01:24:31.100
well in 2006 or in 2005,
Russia did this and this,

01:24:31.530 --> 01:24:33.560
you know, I would say okay, fine.

01:24:33.560 --> 01:24:36.343
I don't know what, you know,
what are we talking about.

01:24:37.180 --> 01:24:42.180
And of course, Ukraine, with
its proximity to Russia,

01:24:46.070 --> 01:24:51.070
and have having been seen,
always as close to Russia

01:24:52.860 --> 01:24:57.043
because of the language,
because of the religion,

01:24:59.210 --> 01:25:04.210
in a way the Russians, you
know, the Rus being where Russia

01:25:04.580 --> 01:25:07.730
was born in Ukraine.

01:25:07.730 --> 01:25:10.510
So the fact that Ukraine would leave,

01:25:10.510 --> 01:25:12.610
very painful to Russians,

01:25:12.610 --> 01:25:15.020
as it would be in, you
know, in other countries,

01:25:15.020 --> 01:25:18.500
but that certainly does
not, how should I this,

01:25:18.500 --> 01:25:21.407
that certainly does
not make Putin blame us

01:25:27.910 --> 01:25:30.180
in what's happened in Ukraine.

01:25:30.180 --> 01:25:32.763
So I have no argument with you at all.

01:25:34.430 --> 01:25:37.760
But because of the
mistakes that we're making,

01:25:37.760 --> 01:25:41.820
in my opinion that were
made, you have now a man

01:25:41.820 --> 01:25:44.763
who has a very different
outlook than he did.

01:25:46.140 --> 01:25:50.953
How many years ago now, you
know, and that's too bad.

01:25:52.430 --> 01:25:55.153
I think that there was a
huge window of opportunity.

01:25:56.100 --> 01:25:57.000
And it was missed.

01:25:58.130 --> 01:25:59.630
And it's too bad for everyone.

01:26:02.257 --> 01:26:03.807
- There's a hand right up here.

01:26:05.450 --> 01:26:07.750
- [Anastasia] Hi, my name is
Anastasia, I'm from Moscow.

01:26:07.750 --> 01:26:10.880
So I grew up in Russia,
but I've lived in the U.S.

01:26:10.880 --> 01:26:12.180
now for five years.

01:26:12.180 --> 01:26:16.170
So we've talked a lot about
Putin and how powerful he is

01:26:16.170 --> 01:26:18.430
and his international policy.

01:26:18.430 --> 01:26:22.650
But, you know, being the
Russian myself, I asked myself,

01:26:22.650 --> 01:26:24.180
is there a future for Russia?

01:26:24.180 --> 01:26:26.530
Should we hope for anything better?

01:26:26.530 --> 01:26:29.810
Should we because you
know, now, I feel like

01:26:29.810 --> 01:26:33.470
there is a big division in
terms of what people think

01:26:33.470 --> 01:26:36.100
like some people just
think Putin is everything,

01:26:36.100 --> 01:26:40.270
all we've got, and there
is no other future for us.

01:26:40.270 --> 01:26:42.630
But then, of course, with
Navalny being in the picture,

01:26:42.630 --> 01:26:44.030
and I know you're not the fan of him.

01:26:44.030 --> 01:26:45.000
- No, I'm not.

01:26:45.000 --> 01:26:47.840
- [Anastasia] But, you know,
except for him, of course,

01:26:47.840 --> 01:26:48.900
there might be someone else.

01:26:48.900 --> 01:26:52.810
But my question is, should
we hope for any improvements

01:26:52.810 --> 01:26:54.950
in terms of like for us for Russians?

01:26:54.950 --> 01:26:57.170
Should we hope for the future for Russia

01:26:57.170 --> 01:26:59.550
or should we just practice
our American accent

01:26:59.550 --> 01:27:00.400
and forget about?

01:27:02.320 --> 01:27:06.200
- Well, you know, when
you asked that question,

01:27:06.200 --> 01:27:08.300
I would have to ask what what do you mean?

01:27:09.620 --> 01:27:11.263
What would you like to change?

01:27:12.410 --> 01:27:17.203
What is it that that makes
you ask that question?

01:27:19.170 --> 01:27:22.630
And my answer to that is,
of course, there's a future.

01:27:22.630 --> 01:27:27.060
And the future is, let me
back up a little bit here.

01:27:27.060 --> 01:27:32.010
The main problem, from my
point of view in Russia,

01:27:32.010 --> 01:27:37.010
is that the leadership, all
the leadership is Soviet.

01:27:39.790 --> 01:27:43.870
All the people who are
at the head of politics

01:27:43.870 --> 01:27:46.120
and all the rest of
it, they're all Soviet.

01:27:46.120 --> 01:27:48.013
They were born in the Soviet Union.

01:27:48.990 --> 01:27:50.370
They went to Soviet schools.

01:27:50.370 --> 01:27:52.780
They were members of the Young Pioneers.

01:27:52.780 --> 01:27:55.270
They were members of the
Young Communist League.

01:27:55.270 --> 01:27:58.020
They were members of the
Communist Party, most of them.

01:28:03.610 --> 01:28:08.610
They were created, formed by a
system that no longer exists.

01:28:12.160 --> 01:28:14.640
They're trying to run a system

01:28:14.640 --> 01:28:17.490
that they really don't
know anything about.

01:28:17.490 --> 01:28:19.090
And they're not very good at it.

01:28:19.960 --> 01:28:24.210
But whatever they do, is
based on the mentality

01:28:24.210 --> 01:28:25.060
that they have.

01:28:25.060 --> 01:28:28.100
And that mentality is a Soviet mentality.

01:28:28.100 --> 01:28:30.250
And there's not much
you can do about that.

01:28:31.190 --> 01:28:33.243
That's the way they were brought up.

01:28:34.240 --> 01:28:36.030
And you can't say, well,
where are the others

01:28:36.030 --> 01:28:37.493
'cause there are no others.

01:28:38.820 --> 01:28:43.820
And what I say is, in my
opinion, the hope is that

01:28:44.610 --> 01:28:47.743
people who are not born
in the Soviet Union,

01:28:48.790 --> 01:28:51.380
who were born afterwards, that is to say,

01:28:51.380 --> 01:28:55.370
the next generation and
the generation after that,

01:28:55.370 --> 01:28:57.800
that's when you will see real change

01:28:58.800 --> 01:29:00.770
and you have to have patience.

01:29:00.770 --> 01:29:03.120
I have no doubt that
Russia is a great country.

01:29:04.470 --> 01:29:06.100
And you know, during the worst times

01:29:06.100 --> 01:29:10.420
like the Tsar Nicholas
I, during those years,

01:29:10.420 --> 01:29:12.703
Russia's greatest literature developed.

01:29:13.660 --> 01:29:17.070
So it's not that, it's not
that you can't just look at it

01:29:17.070 --> 01:29:21.223
from that side, I certainly
hope, I won't see it.

01:29:22.660 --> 01:29:26.343
Not at my age, it's gonna
take another 20, 30 years.

01:29:27.550 --> 01:29:30.340
But in all countries,
for democracy to develop,

01:29:30.340 --> 01:29:31.603
it took over 200.

01:29:33.640 --> 01:29:35.520
And I guess there were
people back then saying,

01:29:35.520 --> 01:29:36.770
gee, do we have a future?

01:29:38.130 --> 01:29:39.540
Because there were all kinds of things.

01:29:39.540 --> 01:29:41.410
You had slavery in the United States

01:29:41.410 --> 01:29:42.943
less than 200 years ago.

01:29:44.250 --> 01:29:47.773
Come on, since you have to
look at it realistically.

01:29:49.490 --> 01:29:53.960
So my answer to you is yes,
I absolutely do believe it.

01:29:53.960 --> 01:29:55.440
But it's going to take time

01:29:55.440 --> 01:29:57.453
and you cannot do anything about that.

01:29:58.370 --> 01:30:01.300
Except that if you're young,

01:30:01.300 --> 01:30:05.730
you can look forward to a
change that is inevitable

01:30:06.610 --> 01:30:08.683
because those people will disappear.

01:30:09.810 --> 01:30:11.260
And it will be your ballgame.

01:30:13.040 --> 01:30:15.492
As a Yankee fan, I know what I'm saying.

01:30:15.492 --> 01:30:18.492
(audience laughing)

01:30:19.430 --> 01:30:22.607
- [Alec] I'm here, here, over there.

01:30:22.607 --> 01:30:23.453
- Oh, okay.

01:30:24.800 --> 01:30:27.030
- I'm a father of Yale undergrad.

01:30:27.030 --> 01:30:30.330
My name is Alec, we met 16
years ago in Kazakhstan.

01:30:30.330 --> 01:30:32.990
We spent a couple of days
in much media business.

01:30:32.990 --> 01:30:33.923
Remember that?

01:30:35.750 --> 01:30:40.613
But the question that
I have, and you know,

01:30:41.530 --> 01:30:43.980
we had very interesting
discussion 16 years ago

01:30:43.980 --> 01:30:48.580
about what made my family
move to America from Odessa.

01:30:48.580 --> 01:30:53.340
And, you know, and I actually
I saw a lot about it,

01:30:53.340 --> 01:30:55.243
and now I know my answer.

01:30:56.760 --> 01:31:01.160
But back then, I believe
I told you the story

01:31:01.160 --> 01:31:04.970
about my father who really, you know,

01:31:04.970 --> 01:31:07.040
after the conflict and Transnistria

01:31:07.040 --> 01:31:10.010
that was 100 kilometers from Odessa,

01:31:10.010 --> 01:31:12.760
when people all of a sudden
started killing each other.

01:31:14.200 --> 01:31:17.100
You know, geopolitics, yeah,
we all understand that.

01:31:17.100 --> 01:31:18.963
Tit for tat, we understand that.

01:31:20.620 --> 01:31:25.210
You know, the United States
and Mexico, Russia and Ukraine,

01:31:25.210 --> 01:31:28.300
Southern border, NATO
fleet and Sevastopol,

01:31:28.300 --> 01:31:29.700
we all understand that.

01:31:29.700 --> 01:31:32.100
But with people going and
killing each other right now

01:31:32.100 --> 01:31:35.460
in 10 years, people who
lived on same street,

01:31:35.460 --> 01:31:39.080
it's not even Karabakh,
it's not like even Armenians

01:31:39.080 --> 01:31:43.510
and Azerbaijanis, we have
some ethnic conflicts, right?

01:31:43.510 --> 01:31:46.810
They have some, you know,
religious differences.

01:31:46.810 --> 01:31:49.020
But whatever is going
on in Donbass right now

01:31:49.020 --> 01:31:52.663
when neighbors killing
neighbors over nothing,

01:31:53.630 --> 01:31:57.090
I mean, do you really
think that that whole issue

01:31:57.090 --> 01:32:00.803
of Ukrainian language is
like really pressing there.

01:32:02.042 --> 01:32:05.210
Or these stories that were made up about,

01:32:05.210 --> 01:32:08.740
you know, Western Ukrainians,
Banderas, you know,

01:32:08.740 --> 01:32:12.203
Nazis was gonna move in,
who's gonna, you know,

01:32:13.120 --> 01:32:14.870
how do we stop that?

01:32:14.870 --> 01:32:17.610
How do we stop people
from killing each other

01:32:17.610 --> 01:32:20.270
in situations like
that, because, you know,

01:32:20.270 --> 01:32:23.430
they can meet up in Minsk,
they can do whatever,

01:32:23.430 --> 01:32:25.400
you know, they can make agreements.

01:32:25.400 --> 01:32:29.490
They can disagree, you
know, better than me about,

01:32:29.490 --> 01:32:32.870
you know, money interest and cryptocracy

01:32:32.870 --> 01:32:35.130
that is so omnipresent in Russia

01:32:35.130 --> 01:32:37.810
and Ukraine, and everywhere.

01:32:37.810 --> 01:32:39.440
But how do we stop that?

01:32:39.440 --> 01:32:40.310
How do we stop?

01:32:40.310 --> 01:32:44.780
How can United States and
Germany and Russia get together

01:32:44.780 --> 01:32:46.943
and stop brothers from killing brothers?

01:32:48.920 --> 01:32:52.940
- Well, you know, if I had the answers

01:32:52.940 --> 01:32:54.950
to all of those things,

01:32:54.950 --> 01:32:57.073
I probably would be in a different job.

01:32:57.970 --> 01:33:02.260
No, seriously, I mean, I
can't tell you how to solve

01:33:03.470 --> 01:33:04.540
the world's problems.

01:33:04.540 --> 01:33:07.170
And, you know, people
are killing each other.

01:33:07.170 --> 01:33:10.030
In many places in Africa, for instance,

01:33:10.030 --> 01:33:11.617
brother's killing brother and so on.

01:33:11.617 --> 01:33:13.680
And this is going on everywhere.

01:33:13.680 --> 01:33:18.680
But I would say that if the
leaders of Russia, of Ukraine,

01:33:21.100 --> 01:33:23.810
of the United States, of Germany,

01:33:23.810 --> 01:33:26.173
we're actually asking that question.

01:33:27.870 --> 01:33:29.950
That question they were asking.

01:33:29.950 --> 01:33:31.900
I think they'd find a way to answer it.

01:33:32.740 --> 01:33:35.300
But I don't think they're
asking that question.

01:33:35.300 --> 01:33:37.700
I think they're asking
very different questions.

01:33:38.560 --> 01:33:40.423
And they have very different aims.

01:33:42.100 --> 01:33:43.853
And that's why this is going on.

01:33:45.070 --> 01:33:49.520
So, to me, the answer is pretty obvious.

01:33:49.520 --> 01:33:50.800
How you make people do that,

01:33:50.800 --> 01:33:52.260
that's a different question.

01:33:52.260 --> 01:33:57.260
Why is it that egoistic
due to political interests

01:34:01.870 --> 01:34:04.410
take first place over these things.

01:34:04.410 --> 01:34:05.950
That's the real question.

01:34:05.950 --> 01:34:07.880
And when you say we understand,

01:34:07.880 --> 01:34:10.070
I don't know who you're talking about.

01:34:10.070 --> 01:34:13.200
Most people don't even know,
you know, in this country,

01:34:13.200 --> 01:34:17.683
where Georgia is for instance,
except the state of Georgia.

01:34:18.720 --> 01:34:21.769
Most people are really
really and in Russia too,

01:34:21.769 --> 01:34:26.769
the level of knowledge is lower

01:34:30.780 --> 01:34:35.780
than the doorstep as they say.

01:34:36.490 --> 01:34:41.490
So there is no effort and
the media does not play

01:34:43.650 --> 01:34:44.560
that game at all.

01:34:44.560 --> 01:34:47.090
It doesn't say, how do we stop?

01:34:47.090 --> 01:34:48.823
It says they are to blame.

01:34:50.540 --> 01:34:52.363
And that's it, and on both sides.

01:34:54.560 --> 01:34:57.910
So I think the only way to
stop it is for us to talk,

01:34:57.910 --> 01:34:59.323
make our voices heard.

01:35:00.930 --> 01:35:02.120
And that's not easy.

01:35:02.120 --> 01:35:03.663
And sometimes it's dangerous.

01:35:04.910 --> 01:35:08.030
But again, if you know, did I ever,

01:35:08.030 --> 01:35:08.980
no, of course not.

01:35:08.980 --> 01:35:11.872
There was a guy by the name,
his last name was Rindskopf,

01:35:11.872 --> 01:35:14.770
he is a German who fought
during World War I,

01:35:14.770 --> 01:35:19.770
he was the youngest Japanese,
U-boat commander or U-boat,

01:35:20.100 --> 01:35:22.430
as they were called, submarines.

01:35:22.430 --> 01:35:27.430
And when Germany lost the war,
he was totally crushed by it.

01:35:27.450 --> 01:35:31.680
And he became a priest,
Protestant priest in Hamburg

01:35:33.690 --> 01:35:35.280
and very anti war.

01:35:35.280 --> 01:35:40.280
And as he went up the ladder
as it were, Hitler did too.

01:35:42.620 --> 01:35:46.070
And Hitler became the head of
Germany and he became the head

01:35:46.070 --> 01:35:49.400
of the Protestant church in
Hamburg at the same time.

01:35:49.400 --> 01:35:51.160
And he spoke out against war.

01:35:51.160 --> 01:35:52.830
And finally he was arrested,

01:35:52.830 --> 01:35:55.510
and he was sent to a concentration camp.

01:35:55.510 --> 01:35:57.740
But he survived.

01:35:57.740 --> 01:35:59.690
And when he came back, he wrote a book.

01:36:01.574 --> 01:36:05.430
And in that book, there's a
passage that I know by heart,

01:36:05.430 --> 01:36:08.680
because I think it's one
of the most important

01:36:08.680 --> 01:36:10.230
and powerful things I've ever heard.

01:36:10.230 --> 01:36:14.287
He wrote, "When they came for the Jews,

01:36:14.287 --> 01:36:16.993
"I didn't say anything,
because I'm not Jewish.

01:36:18.561 --> 01:36:20.527
"And when they came for the communists,

01:36:20.527 --> 01:36:24.427
"I didn't say anything,
because I'm not a communist.

01:36:24.427 --> 01:36:27.047
"And when they came to
the trade union members,

01:36:27.047 --> 01:36:29.487
"I didn't say anything,
because I'm not a member

01:36:29.487 --> 01:36:30.827
"of the trade union.

01:36:30.827 --> 01:36:32.977
"And when I came for the Catholics,

01:36:32.977 --> 01:36:36.237
"I didn't say anything
because I'm not a Catholic.

01:36:36.237 --> 01:36:39.807
"And when they came for me,
there was no one to speak."

01:36:42.960 --> 01:36:45.630
And I feel that, I very
much feel that way.

01:36:45.630 --> 01:36:49.270
If we don't speak out, however we can,

01:36:49.270 --> 01:36:54.270
and not irresponsibly, and not
to say oh, look look at me,

01:36:54.420 --> 01:36:55.990
but rather with this understanding

01:36:55.990 --> 01:37:00.990
that it's our human duty to do this,

01:37:01.070 --> 01:37:03.723
well, then we keep saying,
well, they should do it.

01:37:05.120 --> 01:37:07.560
That's, it's certainly very Russian.

01:37:07.560 --> 01:37:08.460
They should do it.

01:37:09.800 --> 01:37:11.250
And if they don't, then what?

01:37:13.050 --> 01:37:15.093
So that's really, that's my view.

01:37:16.386 --> 01:37:17.910
And it's an idealistic view, perhaps,

01:37:17.910 --> 01:37:19.510
but that's the way I look at it.

01:37:22.840 --> 01:37:25.170
- [Anastasia] Okay, hi, I'm here.

01:37:25.170 --> 01:37:26.605
So my name is--

01:37:26.605 --> 01:37:27.860
- (mumbles) to the left.
- Here.

01:37:27.860 --> 01:37:30.549
Coincidentally, I'm also
Anastasia from Moscow.

01:37:30.549 --> 01:37:32.070
(audience laughing)

01:37:32.070 --> 01:37:35.570
Hats of us here, I'm
a PhD student at Yale.

01:37:35.570 --> 01:37:39.890
So you started your talk by
outlining to strategists,

01:37:39.890 --> 01:37:43.330
the U.S. could have taken
towards the Soviet Union--

01:37:43.330 --> 01:37:44.170
- Towards Russia.

01:37:44.170 --> 01:37:47.390
- Towards Russia, after
the Soviet Union collapsed,

01:37:47.390 --> 01:37:51.370
and presented this as a
cornerstone of the situation

01:37:51.370 --> 01:37:53.610
we have right now.

01:37:53.610 --> 01:37:54.443
- Yeah.

01:37:54.443 --> 01:37:57.980
- So if we hypothesize with your knowledge

01:37:57.980 --> 01:38:01.580
of Russian politics, economy,
you know, the Soviet mentality

01:38:01.580 --> 01:38:05.650
that you mentioned, do you
believe that some version

01:38:05.650 --> 01:38:10.630
of Marshall Plan could
have ever changed anything?

01:38:10.630 --> 01:38:12.460
- Well, you know, that's
a question that regardless

01:38:12.460 --> 01:38:17.403
of how I answer, I can't prove
it 'cause it didn't happen.

01:38:18.610 --> 01:38:21.800
So it's kind of as you say,
it's very hypothetical.

01:38:21.800 --> 01:38:25.500
But do I think that that
would have been better

01:38:25.500 --> 01:38:26.673
than what they did?

01:38:27.640 --> 01:38:32.330
Yes, clearly, clearly, it
would have been better.

01:38:32.330 --> 01:38:35.060
To what extent it would
have failed, who knows?

01:38:35.060 --> 01:38:36.260
I don't know.

01:38:36.260 --> 01:38:40.263
But I do know that when
Gorbachev announced glossiness,

01:38:41.730 --> 01:38:44.387
the media in Russia changed dramatically.

01:38:44.387 --> 01:38:47.410
And I think nobody would argue with that.

01:38:47.410 --> 01:38:52.230
Dramatically overnight, it
became a different kind of media.

01:38:52.230 --> 01:38:57.220
During the Soviet times,
40 million people tuned

01:38:57.220 --> 01:39:00.710
into the Voice of America in Russia,

01:39:00.710 --> 01:39:03.100
The BBC World Service in Russian,

01:39:03.100 --> 01:39:05.820
the Deutsche Welle in Russian,

01:39:05.820 --> 01:39:10.490
because they were not getting
in their own media information

01:39:10.490 --> 01:39:13.370
that they were getting
on these wave, you know,

01:39:13.370 --> 01:39:16.090
and the Russians tried to block that.

01:39:16.090 --> 01:39:20.470
The Soviet government did,
spent trillions of rubles,

01:39:20.470 --> 01:39:24.490
trying to block them but
people listened nonetheless.

01:39:24.490 --> 01:39:27.800
As soon as glossiness came,
and they stopped listening,

01:39:27.800 --> 01:39:29.320
they were getting it
from their own source.

01:39:29.320 --> 01:39:32.590
They were getting the
information, people know exactly

01:39:32.590 --> 01:39:34.930
when they're getting any when they're not.

01:39:34.930 --> 01:39:35.880
Or usually they do.

01:39:36.910 --> 01:39:41.280
So that's one example of
how quickly things change.

01:39:41.280 --> 01:39:44.940
So I do think that had
there been this attitude,

01:39:44.940 --> 01:39:45.893
let's help them.

01:39:46.840 --> 01:39:49.470
Let's help them become democratic.

01:39:49.470 --> 01:39:52.520
I do think that it would
have been a positive,

01:39:52.520 --> 01:39:54.290
the result would have been positive.

01:39:54.290 --> 01:39:55.873
I can't tell you how positive.

01:39:56.880 --> 01:39:59.400
So that's, you know,
my hypothetical answer

01:39:59.400 --> 01:40:01.363
to your hypothetical question.

01:40:02.230 --> 01:40:04.123
- I think we have two more,

01:40:04.123 --> 01:40:06.850
time for two more questions,
perhaps, over here.

01:40:06.850 --> 01:40:09.210
- [Acia] Thank you, my name is Acia.

01:40:09.210 --> 01:40:13.453
I'm from Yerevan, Armenia
right there on the map.

01:40:17.070 --> 01:40:18.550
- Well--

01:40:18.550 --> 01:40:19.880
- [Acia] Yeah, it is tiny,
but it's still there.

01:40:19.880 --> 01:40:21.120
- You see it over there, right.

01:40:21.120 --> 01:40:24.660
That tiny little country
that says Armenia?

01:40:24.660 --> 01:40:25.814
- [Acia] Yes.

01:40:25.814 --> 01:40:27.060
- Okay.

01:40:27.060 --> 01:40:29.860
- [Acia] So I'm a doctoral
student and I study genocides,

01:40:29.860 --> 01:40:30.797
but today I'm going to ask--

01:40:30.797 --> 01:40:31.890
- You study what?

01:40:31.890 --> 01:40:32.810
- [Acia] Genocides.

01:40:32.810 --> 01:40:33.643
- Wow!

01:40:33.643 --> 01:40:34.940
- [Acia] yeah, a lot of fun.

01:40:35.850 --> 01:40:39.950
But today, I'm going to ask a question

01:40:39.950 --> 01:40:41.750
about current developments

01:40:41.750 --> 01:40:43.923
that happened right next door to Russia,

01:40:44.860 --> 01:40:48.030
and are very connected to what you talked,

01:40:48.030 --> 01:40:53.030
about speaking out the new
generation that can change,

01:40:54.270 --> 01:40:57.830
the generation that maybe
does not have this mentality

01:40:57.830 --> 01:40:59.947
can change or may be the change.

01:40:59.947 --> 01:41:00.780
- Yeah.

01:41:00.780 --> 01:41:03.510
- And something like that
recently happened in Armenia,

01:41:03.510 --> 01:41:06.720
no one ever expected that
peaceful demonstrations

01:41:06.720 --> 01:41:10.440
in such a tiny post
Soviet landlocked country

01:41:10.440 --> 01:41:15.440
could lead to change, which was
the collapse of the old rule

01:41:16.210 --> 01:41:19.250
and establishment of the government,

01:41:19.250 --> 01:41:22.430
which is totally and fully
supported by the people.

01:41:22.430 --> 01:41:23.263
- Yeah.

01:41:23.263 --> 01:41:26.570
- And the funny question
that I always hear

01:41:26.570 --> 01:41:31.570
when I discuss this with
students or at conferences,

01:41:32.670 --> 01:41:37.670
is how come Russia did not intervene

01:41:37.960 --> 01:41:41.520
into these developments in any way?

01:41:41.520 --> 01:41:46.520
And I would really appreciate
your opinion in this,

01:41:47.140 --> 01:41:51.657
like, how come that Vladimir
Putin did not really respond

01:41:55.090 --> 01:42:00.090
in any visual way to this development?

01:42:00.350 --> 01:42:05.350
And how was this even or was it observed,

01:42:06.340 --> 01:42:09.860
seen in Russia because it was next door.

01:42:09.860 --> 01:42:13.770
It was a big change.

01:42:13.770 --> 01:42:18.770
And some people were even
speculating how this could serve

01:42:18.860 --> 01:42:20.930
as an example for other countries

01:42:20.930 --> 01:42:24.363
to topple their governments,
et cetera, thank you.

01:42:28.060 --> 01:42:32.270
- Well, one reason might be that Putin

01:42:32.270 --> 01:42:34.673
isn't quite the man you all think he is.

01:42:35.790 --> 01:42:37.660
You know, that's a possibility,

01:42:37.660 --> 01:42:38.983
I'm not insisting on it.

01:42:41.490 --> 01:42:45.680
Another reason might be that
the new leader of Armenia,

01:42:45.680 --> 01:42:49.470
never expressed any
anti Russian sentiment.

01:42:49.470 --> 01:42:53.950
On the contrary, said we are
in an independent country,

01:42:53.950 --> 01:42:55.173
but look where we are.

01:42:56.900 --> 01:43:00.160
And we've always had very
close ties with Russia

01:43:00.160 --> 01:43:03.553
and we wanna have close
ties, economic ties.

01:43:05.170 --> 01:43:09.630
There was no absolutely no
sense of enmity on the contrary,

01:43:12.920 --> 01:43:15.450
And it was the people, there
was no doubt at this time,

01:43:15.450 --> 01:43:17.630
that was the people who spoke out.

01:43:17.630 --> 01:43:20.340
You know, Armenia is really
a very small country.

01:43:20.340 --> 01:43:21.763
What's the population now?

01:43:23.030 --> 01:43:27.400
Three million, and there was a
million people that came out.

01:43:27.400 --> 01:43:31.510
Can you imagine 30% of
the entire population

01:43:31.510 --> 01:43:33.770
comes out in the streets.

01:43:33.770 --> 01:43:34.973
You can't ignore that.

01:43:36.410 --> 01:43:37.710
And that's another reason.

01:43:39.040 --> 01:43:41.850
But the very question,
why didn't Putin interfere

01:43:41.850 --> 01:43:45.270
reflects the way people think about it.

01:43:45.270 --> 01:43:47.953
Because that's the way it's been depicted.

01:43:50.220 --> 01:43:54.523
So, you know, that's my
answer, incidentally.

01:43:56.330 --> 01:43:58.233
Well no, I'm not gonna go into that.

01:43:59.590 --> 01:44:00.770
One more question you say.

01:44:00.770 --> 01:44:02.580
- I think one more, yeah,
we'll try to wrap up by six.

01:44:02.580 --> 01:44:05.200
So we're back on this side.

01:44:05.200 --> 01:44:07.610
I see a hand right next to you.

01:44:07.610 --> 01:44:08.443
Hello, you.

01:44:09.310 --> 01:44:11.220
- [Woman] Hi, I'm a
master's student in European

01:44:11.220 --> 01:44:13.050
and Russian studies.

01:44:13.050 --> 01:44:14.910
I guess my question is about the poisoning

01:44:14.910 --> 01:44:16.747
in the UK earlier this year.

01:44:16.747 --> 01:44:17.580
(audience laughing)

01:44:17.580 --> 01:44:18.673
- Finally, finally.

01:44:21.330 --> 01:44:24.400
- [Woman] Is there a way for
us to respond to that strongly

01:44:24.400 --> 01:44:29.400
that doesn't escalate, the kind
of confrontational paradigm?

01:44:29.550 --> 01:44:34.550
- Yeah, you know, when this
took place, so I was very,

01:44:42.080 --> 01:44:47.080
I was shocked and I tried to figure out

01:44:48.050 --> 01:44:50.113
for myself some kind of logic.

01:44:50.950 --> 01:44:55.273
Now, you know why we
would do this and why.

01:44:56.890 --> 01:44:59.930
Now, you know, let's
forget about the child,

01:44:59.930 --> 01:45:01.530
I means she's lame, you know,

01:45:01.530 --> 01:45:02.363
she didn't do anything.

01:45:02.363 --> 01:45:07.330
Skripal, he was a military agent, right?

01:45:08.210 --> 01:45:11.880
He worked for the former
GRU, not to the KGB,

01:45:11.880 --> 01:45:14.900
Putin work to the KGB,
he works for the GRU,

01:45:14.900 --> 01:45:16.750
which is military intelligence,

01:45:16.750 --> 01:45:18.717
and they can't stand the KGB,

01:45:19.606 --> 01:45:21.840
and the KGB can't stand
their intelligence.

01:45:21.840 --> 01:45:23.140
That's normal competition.

01:45:24.150 --> 01:45:26.360
So, and he betrayed his country.

01:45:26.360 --> 01:45:28.010
Let's face it, right.

01:45:28.010 --> 01:45:30.210
He went over to the other
side and began to work

01:45:30.210 --> 01:45:31.773
for British intelligence.

01:45:32.930 --> 01:45:36.523
And he was caught, and he was tried.

01:45:38.280 --> 01:45:41.113
And he was sentenced to 13 years.

01:45:42.120 --> 01:45:44.990
Now, I don't know if you're
familiar with what happens

01:45:44.990 --> 01:45:49.990
to spies, who to turn
against their own country,

01:45:50.070 --> 01:45:51.330
and are then caught?

01:45:51.330 --> 01:45:52.880
Well, in wartime, they're shot.

01:45:54.160 --> 01:45:57.010
But in peacetime, well,
it's usually something

01:45:57.010 --> 01:46:02.010
like 30 years, 25, 13 is a weird sentence.

01:46:03.860 --> 01:46:08.470
Not only does he get this
rather short sentence,

01:46:08.470 --> 01:46:12.920
considering, but he's
exchanged for Soviet,

01:46:14.960 --> 01:46:17.023
excuse me, Russian spies who were caught.

01:46:18.280 --> 01:46:20.420
Now if he was exchanged that means

01:46:20.420 --> 01:46:23.565
that he really didn't know
anything at that point.

01:46:23.565 --> 01:46:26.763
He was no danger to the Russian side.

01:46:28.000 --> 01:46:30.450
So, you know, let him go
and we'll get ours back.

01:46:33.291 --> 01:46:37.203
Now, Putin doesn't like traders, who does?

01:46:38.980 --> 01:46:40.623
If Putin wanted to kill him,

01:46:42.690 --> 01:46:45.573
he was in prison, he would do it.

01:46:46.527 --> 01:46:48.530
And you could say that
he had a heart attack

01:46:48.530 --> 01:46:50.900
or that he committed suicide or whatever.

01:46:50.900 --> 01:46:53.853
He was no problem killing
when he was in jail in Russia.

01:46:56.200 --> 01:46:58.120
They let him go, they exchanged him,

01:46:58.120 --> 01:47:00.000
they could have exchanged someone else,

01:47:00.000 --> 01:47:00.950
they exchanged him.

01:47:04.040 --> 01:47:09.000
What sense would it
make to poison this man

01:47:09.940 --> 01:47:13.090
under those circumstances,
I mean logically.

01:47:13.090 --> 01:47:14.913
Putin is anything but stupid.

01:47:15.940 --> 01:47:20.320
Not stupid, it's very risky.

01:47:20.320 --> 01:47:24.500
The risk of somehow this being
found out is always there.

01:47:24.500 --> 01:47:28.190
Why do it, this is not a dangerous person.

01:47:28.190 --> 01:47:30.310
He can't do anything.

01:47:30.310 --> 01:47:33.293
He can talk about what
he knows, but it's over.

01:47:34.630 --> 01:47:37.190
So I try to find, I'm not
saying he didn't do it.

01:47:37.190 --> 01:47:40.920
I'm saying I'm trying to
find some kind of logic,

01:47:40.920 --> 01:47:45.920
logic, not emotions, logic as
to why Putin would be involved

01:47:47.030 --> 01:47:48.590
in something like that.

01:47:48.590 --> 01:47:50.030
Alright, it's not Putin.

01:47:50.030 --> 01:47:55.030
It's one of those lower, you
know, one of the GRU people

01:47:55.420 --> 01:48:00.120
who think that Putin would
like it if they did it

01:48:02.930 --> 01:48:05.283
But would Putin like it
if it was discovered?

01:48:06.350 --> 01:48:08.733
No, of course not, they get
their head chopped down.

01:48:09.820 --> 01:48:11.120
So why would they risk it?

01:48:12.130 --> 01:48:14.633
They're not gonna get
decorations for doing it.

01:48:15.590 --> 01:48:18.520
Because if Putin had ordered it, then yes.

01:48:18.520 --> 01:48:20.190
So why would they do it?

01:48:20.190 --> 01:48:22.363
So to me, it really remains a mystery.

01:48:23.230 --> 01:48:27.320
Because it's stupid,
it's counterproductive.

01:48:27.320 --> 01:48:29.983
It doesn't do anything positive at all.

01:48:32.590 --> 01:48:34.250
So am I denying anything?

01:48:34.250 --> 01:48:37.340
I'm not denying, I'm saying give me proof.

01:48:37.340 --> 01:48:41.520
Please, just show me, yes, there it is.

01:48:41.520 --> 01:48:43.970
Now this interview, did you see it?

01:48:43.970 --> 01:48:46.003
The interview of Rita, what's her name?

01:48:48.050 --> 01:48:51.350
Sonya, of those two two people.

01:48:51.350 --> 01:48:52.183
Did you see it?

01:48:53.130 --> 01:48:57.800
It was one of the most unprofessional

01:48:59.260 --> 01:49:03.618
pieces of work that I've ever seen.

01:49:03.618 --> 01:49:04.770
(audience laughing)

01:49:04.770 --> 01:49:09.450
That was just, you know,
I thought what, who, why?

01:49:09.450 --> 01:49:10.807
And it happened, you
know, the day before that

01:49:10.807 --> 01:49:15.080
Putin had said these two
men should go to the media

01:49:15.080 --> 01:49:18.320
and so they come the next
day, they're there, you know.

01:49:18.320 --> 01:49:22.640
It's laughable except
that it's not, not really.

01:49:22.640 --> 01:49:26.363
So I would say to you that
whole story is disgusting.

01:49:28.390 --> 01:49:33.390
I find it hard to believe
that Putin initiated it,

01:49:33.730 --> 01:49:37.730
I really do, but somebody did.

01:49:37.730 --> 01:49:39.030
There's no doubt about it.

01:49:39.970 --> 01:49:43.420
And now I've heard a really nice story.

01:49:43.420 --> 01:49:47.090
You know that Mr Skripal in Britain,

01:49:47.090 --> 01:49:51.100
every time he'd meet with his
handlers, as they call them,

01:49:51.100 --> 01:49:53.320
he would be paid a
certain amount, not a lot,

01:49:53.320 --> 01:49:57.620
you know, 5,000 pounds or 7,000 pounds,

01:49:57.620 --> 01:49:58.640
where if he felt he had something

01:49:58.640 --> 01:50:01.560
really interesting to say, 10,000 pounds.

01:50:01.560 --> 01:50:04.260
But again, he was of no real interest.

01:50:04.260 --> 01:50:08.800
So I'm thinking, and now
this is John le Carré, right?

01:50:08.800 --> 01:50:13.560
I'm thinking, British intelligence
gets together and says,

01:50:13.560 --> 01:50:16.973
we don't need this guy,
and he's costing us money.

01:50:17.920 --> 01:50:19.180
And he's not kidding.

01:50:19.180 --> 01:50:24.180
Now, why don't we, and make
it look like it's Putin.

01:50:26.710 --> 01:50:31.710
Well, that's brilliant Chap,
you should do this, you know.

01:50:31.930 --> 01:50:34.960
So British intelligence poisons the guy,

01:50:34.960 --> 01:50:37.120
and the whole thing works this way.

01:50:37.120 --> 01:50:40.010
It's not impossible.

01:50:40.010 --> 01:50:43.000
I kind of laughed at myself
when I came up with that one.

01:50:43.000 --> 01:50:46.990
And I'd like to talk to Mr.
le Carré and see how he thinks.

01:50:46.990 --> 01:50:50.147
Incidentally, if you've not
read his last two books,

01:50:50.147 --> 01:50:55.147
"The Pigeon Tunnel," which is
a story of his life as a spy.

01:50:56.690 --> 01:50:59.537
And the other one I
forgot, "Legacy of a Spy."

01:51:03.290 --> 01:51:08.290
He writes with total disgust
about that profession

01:51:08.290 --> 01:51:10.300
that he was in for so long.

01:51:10.300 --> 01:51:11.193
I mean, really.

01:51:13.650 --> 01:51:16.340
So, you know, there it is.

01:51:16.340 --> 01:51:19.730
My answer to you is, I just don't know.

01:51:19.730 --> 01:51:23.230
It's such a weird thing, he really is.

01:51:23.230 --> 01:51:24.410
I see no reason for it.

01:51:24.410 --> 01:51:28.400
It's just invading another
country I can explain, right.

01:51:28.400 --> 01:51:29.793
But this I don't know.

01:51:30.850 --> 01:51:31.973
It makes no sense.

01:51:33.200 --> 01:51:35.390
One day, we'll know the real thing.

01:51:35.390 --> 01:51:36.987
Always have, is always very, I don't know,

01:51:36.987 --> 01:51:38.370
if you've ever been there.

01:51:38.370 --> 01:51:42.400
I was there, I shot a
documentary about England.

01:51:42.400 --> 01:51:46.280
And so, you know why I went
there, not because the steeple

01:51:46.280 --> 01:51:49.320
is I don't know, how many
feet high or something.

01:51:49.320 --> 01:51:50.590
I have no idea.

01:51:50.590 --> 01:51:55.060
It's a beautiful,
beautiful church actually.

01:51:55.060 --> 01:51:59.530
But it's because they have
their one of the three

01:51:59.530 --> 01:52:02.293
original copies of the Magna Carta.

01:52:04.080 --> 01:52:06.770
And that's the beginning of
real democracy in the West,

01:52:06.770 --> 01:52:10.000
if you don't count the ancient Greeks,

01:52:10.000 --> 01:52:11.573
and I really wanted to see it.

01:52:12.661 --> 01:52:13.494
And they brought it out.

01:52:13.494 --> 01:52:16.450
And I can't tell you what, you
know, when you look at that.

01:52:16.450 --> 01:52:20.720
And you say, what was it 12,
15 and they were already saying

01:52:20.720 --> 01:52:23.930
you can't put a man in
prison without proving

01:52:23.930 --> 01:52:25.330
that he's committed a crime.

01:52:26.600 --> 01:52:29.370
And you have all the kings
and queens and all of that.

01:52:29.370 --> 01:52:31.870
It's amazing, that's why I went there.

01:52:31.870 --> 01:52:34.230
These guys didn't even
know it was there, right?

01:52:34.230 --> 01:52:37.431
They went to see the
steeple, I mean, come on.

01:52:37.431 --> 01:52:38.264
- All right--

01:52:38.264 --> 01:52:41.687
- Let me thank everyone
for listening to me.

01:52:41.687 --> 01:52:44.854
(audience applauding)

01:52:54.131 --> 01:52:56.714
(gentle music)

